window.__CS249R_CHAPTERS__ = window.__CS249R_CHAPTERS__ || {};
window.__CS249R_CHAPTERS__["vol3/18_multi_agent"] = {"key": "vol3/18_multi_agent", "sections": [{"id": "sec-1", "level": 2, "title": "Purpose"}, {"id": "sec-2", "level": 2, "title": "Splitting the Repair Agent's Work"}, {"id": "sec-3", "level": 2, "title": "The Delegation Trade-Off"}, {"id": "sec-4", "level": 3, "title": "Motivations for delegation"}, {"id": "sec-5", "level": 3, "title": "The coordination tax"}, {"id": "sec-6", "level": 3, "title": "Static plans and dynamic spawning"}, {"id": "sec-7", "level": 2, "title": "Optimistic Concurrency Control"}, {"id": "sec-8", "level": 3, "title": "Shared workspace hazards"}, {"id": "sec-9", "level": 3, "title": "Credit-based flow control"}, {"id": "sec-10", "level": 3, "title": "Scaling models for worker count"}, {"id": "sec-11", "level": 3, "title": "A complete delegation decision"}], "html": "<h1 id=\"sec-1\" class=\"cs-h1\">Multi-Agent Coordination</h1>\n<h2 id=\"sec-1\" class=\"cs-h2\">Purpose</h2>\n<p><em>Why can splitting a task across several agents cost more and deliver less than giving one agent the same resources?</em></p>\n<p>Splitting a task across several agents looks like the natural answer when the work is wide, since each worker can hold a smaller context and pursue its piece in parallel. The split replaces nothing the single agent already had. A capable single controller already runs searches and tests concurrently, keeps its context lean through retrieval and compaction, and holds scoped grants, so delegation must be judged against that baseline rather than against a fragile loop. What delegation adds is a set of boundaries between separately managed trajectories, and each boundary must carry explicitly what one trajectory carried implicitly. A worker can build against an interface that changes before its edit lands, two patches can merge cleanly and still break each other, reviewers that share a model can agree on the same mistake, and a canceled child can leave a container running and a budget half spent. Coordination overhead grows with every worker added, while the serial part of the task caps any speedup the parallel part can buy. Whether to split is therefore a measured decision. A multi-agent design earns its place only when it delivers more accepted tasks per unit of cost, or finishes them sooner in a way the objective values, than a single controller given the same tools and budget. Every handoff multiplies horizon, state, and authority, and the runtime must keep each delegated obligation owned until it settles.</p>\n<div class=\"cs-callout\"><div class=\"cs-callout-title\">Learning Objectives</div>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>Estimate the speedup and useful worker count of a work partition from its serial fraction and coordination overhead</li>\n<li>Calculate makespan bounds from a task's dependency graph to decide whether added slots or shorter tasks pay</li>\n<li>Design bounded receipts and credit windows that cap returned context and payload occupancy, and size mean residence under an explicit freshness policy</li>\n<li>Apply shared-write collision estimates to justify private workspaces and dependency revalidation before publication</li>\n<li>Calculate majority false approval under correlated reviewer errors to choose between more reviewers and a different check</li>\n<li>Evaluate fan-out completion rules and hedging under deadline, capacity, and duplicate-effect constraints</li>\n<li>Apply spawn limits, restart policies, attenuated grants, and atomic reservations to bound a delegation tree's fan-out, authority, and spending</li>\n<li>Evaluate delegation against a capable single-controller baseline using a declared objective and matched resource ceilings</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<h2 id=\"sec-2\" class=\"cs-h2\">Splitting the Repair Agent's Work</h2>\n<p>The repair agent (@sec-vol3-intro-lighthouses) has so far fixed a connection-pool race, a change confined to one module that a single controller holds comfortably in one context. Its next assignment is wider: migrate an authentication module across a repository, a change that reaches every caller of the module's interface and is exactly the kind of repair task that tempts a split. The task naturally splits into two coupled changes: revising an internal interface and updating its downstream callers. A single coordinator could retain the entire task, dispatching parallel searches and test runs to collect evidence before synthesizing a unified patch. Alternatively, it could partition the problem, assigning the interface update and caller modifications to separate workers. Each worker operates within a focused context window, editing candidate files and reacting to compiler diagnostics independently. This division introduces an immediate distributed consistency challenge: the caller modifications must remain compatible with the interface that ultimately lands, not merely the snapshot the caller worker read at dispatch.</p>\n<p>The runtime mechanisms established in earlier chapters govern a single execution trajectory at a time. Within that boundary, the harness checks each model and tool invocation against an assigned grant and token budget. Durable execution logs one trajectory's event history, and failure recovery settles, repairs, or compensates the effects of that one trajectory. Evaluation compares trials of one configuration against another, and curation and post-training improve the policy that drives it. Partitioning the migration preserves these local controls inside each individual worker, but it leaves the coordination space between them unmanaged. A single-agent runtime provides no mechanism to resolve which interface revision the caller worker targets, whether a clean textual merge of two patches preserves semantic correctness, or what consensus among five reviewer agents actually guarantees. Nor does it define who claims an orphaned test container after a coordinator cancels a subtask, or how a parent bounds the authority and compute budget delegated to a child. Without explicit systems controls, every such gap threatens to make the split cost far more than it buys.</p>\n<p>Making delegation governable requires runtime controls, and deciding whether it pays requires an empirical framework. The required controls are state records and verification gates owned directly by the runtime rather than left to prompt instructions. They include enforceable task assignments and receipts that separate an agent's self-reported claims from verified tool evidence. They also include a publication barrier that revalidates cross-module dependencies before any candidate patch merges, clean cancellation paths that account for in-flight work, and authority grants that narrow monotonically at every delegation hop. These mechanisms pair with a measurement discipline that compares each multi-agent design against a single agent holding equivalent parallel tools and resource budgets, and that prices every token consumed by failed, retried, or canceled subtasks.</p>\n<p>The migration carries the argument from the decision to split through the final accounting, and each control answers a hazard that the previous one exposes. A screen of serial work against coordination overhead first decides whether a partition can pay at all (@sec-vol3-multiagent-need). Dependency, communication, and supervision structures then fix what may run concurrently and who owns each child (@sec-vol3-multiagent-topologies), and typed envelopes and receipts fix what crosses each handoff (@sec-vol3-multiagent-contracts). Once workers run in parallel, guarded publication keeps their edits consistent (@sec-vol3-multiagent-concurrency), and a joint error model shows what agreement among reviewers can and cannot certify (@sec-vol3-multiagent-consensus). Cancellation, hedging, and credit windows bound the work still in flight (@sec-vol3-multiagent-backpressure), and attenuated grants and atomic reservations bound authority and spending at every hop (@sec-vol3-multiagent-authority). A matched comparison against a capable single controller decides whether the whole design earns its tax (@sec-vol3-multiagent-evaluation).</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h2\">The Delegation Trade-Off</h2>\n<p>Handing the interface update and the caller update to dedicated workers differs from issuing concurrent model queries, and that difference defines the unit of delegation. A <strong>delegated worker</strong> owns a bounded child trajectory with its own context window, model-and-tool loop, task contract, and independent lifecycle. Several candidate samples dispatched by one controller, such as the self-consistency votes of test-time compute, are instead parallel calls inside one trajectory. They are the cheapest ensemble an agent can run and also the most correlated, because every sample shares the model, the prompt, and the input's difficulty, a dependence that @sec-vol3-multiagent-consensus quantifies. The architectural shift lies in establishing explicit boundaries between separately managed trajectories, regardless of whether those workers execute on a single host or across a distributed cluster.</p>\n<p>↩ Self-consistency, which samples several reasoning paths and returns the plurality answer, is established in @sec-vol3-test-time-compute-selection-policies.</p>\n<p>The point of comparison throughout this chapter is the <em>capable baseline</em>: the single-agent runtime developed in earlier chapters, which already manages its context window, reserves system resources, verifies proposed tool effects externally, and executes candidate searches and isolated tools concurrently. Delegation retains every one of those operational requirements while introducing new coordination boundaries. The systems question is whether dividing control and context improves a declared task objective enough to justify the overhead of managing them.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h3\">Motivations for delegation</h3>\n<p>When a single agent runs a wide repository migration inside one shared context and one broad authority grant, compiler diagnostics from one module crowd out the prompt needed for the next while every tool call carries permissions for the whole codebase. <strong>Context partitioning</strong> addresses the prompt growth directly: each worker retains only the material strictly necessary for its subtask. In the migration scenario, the interface worker maintains the target API contract and local unit tests without ingesting the intermediate compiler diagnostics of the caller worker, allowing each subsequent model invocation to process substantially fewer prompt tokens. However, the net token savings depend entirely on the structure of the decomposition. If both workers repeatedly require the full repository commit history, separate contexts duplicate prompt state rather than isolating it, a redundancy the single-agent baseline already avoids through retrieval and compaction.</p>\n<p>Delegation also provides <em>separate task ownership</em>. Workers can pursue multi-turn exploratory investigations through iterative tool feedback and local reasoning loops while a central coordinator tracks inter-task dependencies and orchestrates final integration. Assigning task ownership, however, does not eliminate underlying data dependencies. A caller worker that generates code against an outdated interface must still reconcile its implementation once the interface worker finalizes the new contract. True operational independence derives from the causal graph of the task itself, never from cosmetic role descriptions in prompt templates.</p>\n<p>Finally, decomposing work across workers enforces <em>task-scoped authority</em>, binding tools, execution capabilities, and resource budgets directly to individual subtasks. For example, a test-runner worker can be granted execution privileges in an isolated container without granting it credentials to publish pull requests or write to production databases. Even so, process isolation does not eliminate trust boundaries. A child worker's textual output can still inject unvalidated claims or prompt injections into the parent coordinator's context, and any external mutations it produces must still be verified and settled by the runtime. @Tbl-vol3-delegation-matrix separates these opportunities from capabilities already present in the baseline.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>One controller already provides</th><th>Delegation adds</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Parallel work</td><td>Concurrent candidate calls and tools</td><td>Independently managed, multi-turn subtask trajectories</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Context</td><td>Retrieval, compaction, and selective observations</td><td>Separate working contexts and explicit return boundaries</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Authority and isolation</td><td>Scoped grants and sandboxed execution</td><td>Descendant grants and lifecycle relationships to enforce</td></tr>\n<tr><td>State and evidence</td><td>Versioned artifacts and checks on proposed changes</td><td>Cross-worker dependency validation and integrated-result assessment</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Resource control</td><td>Admission, reservations, deadlines, and settlement</td><td>Aggregate obligations across descendants and their communication</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>What Delegation Adds</strong>: The comparison preserves the capable single-controller runtime and identifies the extra boundaries a coordinated design must justify. {#tbl-vol3-delegation-matrix}</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h3\">The coordination tax</h3>\n<p>Splitting the authentication migration across separate interface and caller workers adds work that a single controller never performs: the coordinator spends turns packaging task assignments and reconciling returned diffs, both workers ingest shared repository context, and final integration waits at the join barrier for the slower child to finish. Every delegation boundary incurs this coordination tax (principle \\ref{pri-vol3-coordination-tax}).</p>\n<p><em><strong>Coordination tax</strong></em> is the work and elapsed time a delegated design spends on handoffs rather than on the task: preparing and validating envelopes, delivering context, scheduling children, collecting receipts, and reconciling and integrating their results.</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Significance</strong>: The tax enters both terms of the cost per accepted task, $\\CostPerAcceptedTask$ (@eq-vol3-cost-per-accepted-task): its tokens and tool time raise $\\mathbb{E}[\\AttemptCost]$, and the part on the critical path lengthens every attempt against its deadline.</li>\n<li><strong>Distinction</strong>: While task decomposition aims to reduce makespan by distributing work concurrently, the coordination tax represents the unavoidable friction introduced by that distribution. This tax scales with worker count, communication frequency, and inter-task dependencies.</li>\n<li><strong>Common pitfall</strong>: Accounting only for the tokens and execution time of successful worker turns. Context re-ingestion, supervisor arbitration turns, failed retries, and abandoned speculative branches all consume model quota and execution budgets.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Coordination tax impacts systems along two distinct dimensions: aggregate resource consumption and end-to-end latency. Duplicated input prompt tokens, redundant tool invocations, and serialize-deserialize cycles increase the total operational cost. Meanwhile, user-perceived delay is governed by <strong>makespan</strong>, the wall-clock time elapsed from the arrival of a task to the completion and verification of its final artifact. Makespan depends strictly on the operations situated on the critical path. A straggling child worker delays synchronization at the join barrier, while subsequent arbitration and reconciliation turns further inflate the critical path before results can be accepted.</p>\n<p>To test whether parallel execution can shorten that makespan despite the coordination tax, Amdahl's fixed-work argument gives the first screening model [@amdahl1967]. Let $\\SerialReferenceTime$ be the time for a fixed set of operations executed serially, with fraction $\\ParallelFraction$ perfectly divisible among $\\WorkerCount$ equally fast workers and fraction $1-\\ParallelFraction$ remaining serial. Assume enough resources for that division and let $\\CoordinationOverhead(\\WorkerCount)$ be the additional elapsed overhead, normalized by $\\SerialReferenceTime$. Then</p>\n<p>$$\\text{Speedup}(\\WorkerCount)=\\frac{\\SerialReferenceTime}{\\Time(\\WorkerCount)}=\\frac{1}{(1-\\ParallelFraction)+\\ParallelFraction/\\WorkerCount+\\CoordinationOverhead(\\WorkerCount)}.$$ {#eq-vol3-multiagent-speedup}</p>\n<p>: <strong>Amdahl's Argument</strong>: Amdahl's 1967 conference paper contains no equation; it argues in prose that the serial housekeeping of a program limits what any number of parallel processors can deliver, and the familiar formula was written down by later authors. The lesson transfers intact to delegation, where planning, integration, and final assessment are the housekeeping that no added worker absorbs.</p>\n<p>With no coordination overhead, $\\CoordinationOverhead(\\WorkerCount)=0$, @eq-vol3-multiagent-speedup reduces to Amdahl's law, and the serial term alone caps speedup at $1/(1-\\ParallelFraction)$ for any number of workers. The equation provides a preliminary screening model for a specified work partition. The baseline $\\SerialReferenceTime$ reflects that partition executed sequentially on a single thread of execution, rather than the capable baseline runtime (which already pipelines tool executions and concurrent candidate samplings). In production, an empirical benchmark of the capable baseline must replace $\\SerialReferenceTime$ before finalizing any worker allocation. Within this theoretical formulation, achieving any net speedup over the serial reference requires that:</p>\n<p>$$\\CoordinationOverhead(\\WorkerCount)&lt;\\ParallelFraction\\left(1-\\frac{1}{\\WorkerCount}\\right).$$</p>\n<p>The right-hand side represents the theoretical wall-clock time saved by distributing the parallelizable portion across $\\WorkerCount$ workers. Whenever coordination overhead $\\CoordinationOverhead(\\WorkerCount)$ exceeds this threshold, the split task takes longer than serial execution. This inequality also constrains execution time alone. Task acceptance rates, token expenditures, and monetary costs enter the trade-off in @sec-vol3-multiagent-evaluation, which gives serializing work and pairwise reconciliation separate coefficients because their remedies differ (@sec-vol3-multiagent-scaling-models).</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ SCREENING A FIXED WORK PARTITION (LEGO)\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Goal: Find the integer worker count minimizing modeled elapsed time.\n# │ Show: Normalized times at four, eight, and the best feasible worker count.\n# │ How:  Enumerate (1-f) + f/M + gamma(M-1) under a finite resource ceiling\n# │       (Scenarios.RepairPartitionScreen and its halved-overhead variant).\n# │ Note: Dimensionless hypothetical assumptions, not a measured comparison\n# │       with an already-parallel single-controller runtime.\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import Scenarios\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import check, fmt_ratio, fmt_int, fmt_multiple\n\nclass DelegationScreen:\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────\n    screen = Scenarios.RepairPartitionScreen\n    halved = Scenarios.RepairPartitionScreenHalvedOverhead\n    parallel_fraction = screen.parallel_fraction\n    overhead_per_extra_worker = screen.overhead_per_extra_worker\n    max_workers = screen.max_workers\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────\n    normalized_times = screen.elapsed\n    best_workers = screen.best_workers\n    best_time = screen.best_time\n    best_speedup = screen.best_speedup\n    speedup_cap = screen.speedup_cap\n    # Sensitivity: halve the per-worker overhead, same serial fraction.\n    halved_overhead = halved.overhead_per_extra_worker\n    halved_best_workers = halved.best_workers\n    halved_best_speedup = halved.best_speedup\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(isinstance(max_workers, int) and max_workers &gt;= 1, &quot;Worker ceiling must be a positive integer&quot;)\n    check(0 &lt;= parallel_fraction &lt;= 1, &quot;Fraction must lie in [0, 1]&quot;)\n    check(overhead_per_extra_worker &gt;= 0, &quot;Overhead cannot be negative&quot;)\n    check(abs(normalized_times[1] - 1) &lt; 1e-12, &quot;Serial reference must be one&quot;)\n    check(best_workers == 6, &quot;Expected six-worker minimum under these assumptions&quot;)\n    check(best_time &lt; normalized_times[5] and best_time &lt; normalized_times[7], &quot;Minimum must beat adjacent integer counts&quot;)\n    check(normalized_times[8] &gt; best_time, &quot;Additional workers should increase time here&quot;)\n    check(halved_best_workers == 9, &quot;Halved overhead should move the optimum to nine workers&quot;)\n    check(best_speedup &lt; halved_best_speedup &lt; speedup_cap, &quot;Halved overhead helps but stays under the serial cap&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────\n    fraction_str = fmt_ratio(parallel_fraction, precision=2)\n    overhead_str = fmt_ratio(overhead_per_extra_worker, precision=2)\n    max_workers_str = fmt_int(max_workers)\n    best_workers_str = fmt_int(best_workers)\n    best_time_str = fmt_ratio(best_time, precision=3)\n    four_time_str = fmt_ratio(normalized_times[4], precision=3)\n    eight_time_str = fmt_ratio(normalized_times[8], precision=3)\n    best_speedup_mult_str = fmt_multiple(best_speedup, precision=1)\n    speedup_cap_mult_str = fmt_multiple(speedup_cap, precision=0)\n    halved_overhead_str = fmt_ratio(halved_overhead, precision=2)\n    halved_best_workers_str = fmt_int(halved_best_workers)\n    halved_best_speedup_mult_str = fmt_multiple(halved_best_speedup, precision=1)\n    added_workers_str = fmt_int(halved_best_workers - best_workers)\n    added_speedup_str = fmt_ratio(halved_best_speedup - best_speedup, precision=1)\n]```\n\n::: {#nbk-17-the-amdahl-limits-of-multi-agent .callout-notebook title=&quot;Screening a proposed work partition&quot;}\n\n**Problem**: A hypothetical fixed workload has $\\ParallelFraction=$ `[DelegationScreen.fraction_str]` and assumed normalized overhead $\\CoordinationOverhead(\\WorkerCount)=\\WorkerOverheadSlope(\\WorkerCount-1)$ with $\\WorkerOverheadSlope=$ `[DelegationScreen.overhead_str]`. The resource ceiling permits at most `[DelegationScreen.max_workers_str]` workers. Which integer count minimizes modeled elapsed time?\n\n**Math**: Evaluating the denominator of @eq-vol3-multiagent-speedup for each permitted count, four workers require `[DelegationScreen.four_time_str]` of the serial reference time. The minimum occurs at `[DelegationScreen.best_workers_str]` workers, requiring `[DelegationScreen.best_time_str]`, a `[DelegationScreen.best_speedup_mult_str]` speedup. Eight workers increase the ratio to `[DelegationScreen.eight_time_str]`, because the shrinking parallel term $\\ParallelFraction/\\WorkerCount$ no longer pays for the added overhead. Halving the overhead coefficient to `[DelegationScreen.halved_overhead_str]` moves the optimum to `[DelegationScreen.halved_best_workers_str]` workers and the speedup to `[DelegationScreen.halved_best_speedup_mult_str]`.\n\n**Systems insight**: The serial fraction sets the ceiling and the overhead sets the useful fleet size. No worker count beats $1/(1-\\ParallelFraction)=$ `[DelegationScreen.speedup_cap_mult_str]` here, and the best count sits near $\\sqrt{\\ParallelFraction/\\WorkerOverheadSlope}$, so halving the per-worker overhead $\\WorkerOverheadSlope$ adds `[DelegationScreen.added_workers_str]` workers but only `[DelegationScreen.added_speedup_str]` of speedup. Shrinking the serial planning and integration work is the larger lever.\n\n:::\n\nA reduction in modeled makespan does not automatically justify a multi-agent architecture. Production systems optimize under varied constraints: one automated migration service may seek to maximize accepted pull requests prior to a nightly release window subject to a strict token budget, whereas another prioritizes minimizing the total cost per accepted migration. Trading latency for computational overhead is justified only against an explicit, pre-declared optimization objective, and the decision framework of @sec-vol3-multiagent-evaluation charges that objective for retries and failed executions as well. Before sizing a worker pool, an engineer must map the causal dependency graph of the workload to see which subtasks can run concurrently and what evidence each downstream join requires. Those dependencies govern the coordination topology.\n\n## Coordination Topologies {#sec-vol3-multiagent-topologies}\n\nIn the authentication migration (`migration_task_01`), the interface worker and the caller worker can inspect the baseline snapshot (`repo_base_01`) in parallel, yet the caller&#x27;s patch still depends on the interface definitions that its peer is actively modifying. Routing both workers through a single supervisor does not eliminate that underlying causal dependency. A runtime architecture that naively treats the supervisor&#x27;s message-routing graph as the true dependency graph will prematurely accept and publish a caller patch built on stale interface assumptions.\n\nTo build reliable multi-agent systems, engineers must distinguish three separate structural graphs that govern execution:\n\n1. The **dependency graph** defines causal precedence by recording which artifacts, decisions, or validation checks must complete before downstream work can safely proceed.\n2. The **communication topology** defines the network paths, protocols, and message envelopes through which agents exchange information.\n3. The **supervision tree** defines operational ownership over agent lifecycles, governing fault isolation, resource reservations, and cancellation cascades.\n\nBecause a supervisor frequently manages children whose outputs depend causally on one another, runtime engines must maintain these three structures independently. Conflating message delivery with causal dependency or lifecycle ownership leads directly to race conditions, orphaned processes, and corrupted state.\n\n### Coordination arrangements {#sec-vol3-multiagent-arrangements}\n\nWhen `migration_task_01` splits work across agents, the runtime must decide whether subtasks and candidate artifacts pass through a central coordinator, stream along staged queues, land in a shared store, or route directly between peer workers (@tbl-vol3-topologies-comparison). The most prevalent pattern is the **supervisor-worker** topology (frequently termed orchestrator-worker). In this model, a centralized coordinator decomposes an incoming task, dispatches subtasks to subordinate workers, and synthesizes their returned receipts. This design directly mirrors the contract net protocol originally developed for distributed problem solving [@smith1980contract]. In production systems, these foundational topologies frequently compose. For example, a supervisor might dispatch parallel analytical investigations, stream their candidate patches through a staged validation pipeline, and persist intermediate progress within a shared task store.\n\n| Arrangement       | How work crosses a boundary                                                           | Useful when                                                              | Obligation that remains                                              |\n|:------------------|:--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|:-------------------------------------------------------------------------|:---------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Supervisor–worker | A coordinator assigns subtasks and collects bounded receipts                          | A task has separable investigations and an explicit integration decision | Bound dispatch, returned context, and the coordinator&#x27;s own work     |\n| Pipeline          | Each stage receives the artifacts and evidence its predecessor is required to provide | Later work depends on a sequence of transformations or checks            | Preserve stage preconditions and bound queues between stages         |\n| Shared blackboard | Workers claim tasks and publish versioned artifacts through a shared store            | Different workers can contribute as dependencies become available        | Enforce claim ownership, publication rules, and dependency validity  |\n| Actor messaging   | Workers hold local state and exchange addressed messages                              | Responsibilities require ongoing asynchronous interactions               | Define delivery, duplicate handling, ordering, and bounded mailboxes |\n\n: **Coordination arrangements and runtime obligations**: While routing patterns structure message handoffs, the execution runtime must enforce result validity, resource boundaries, and state consistency across agent boundaries. {#tbl-vol3-topologies-comparison}\n\nEach coordination topology makes different trade-offs regarding where state accumulates and where synchronization occurs. A supervisor concentrates integration logic and validation evidence at a single coordinator, requiring compact, structured receipts to prevent prompt context exhaustion. A pipeline structures execution as a sequence of discrete transformations, keeping each stage&#x27;s working context tightly focused while streaming items concurrently across stages. By contrast, a shared blackboard decouples agents entirely by externalizing coordination into a centralized artifact and task store [@erman1980hearsay]. In an actor architecture, originally formulated by Hewitt, Bishop, and Steiger, agents maintain completely isolated private state and communicate exclusively via asynchronous message passing to explicit addresses [@hewitt1973actor]. Both blackboards and actor meshes shift the coordination burden toward explicit conflict resolution, distributed ordering, and bounded mailbox management.\n\n: **Contract Net Protocol**: In Smith&#x27;s 1980 protocol, a manager node announces a task, idle contractor nodes submit bids, and the manager awards the contract to a chosen bidder, which may subcontract in turn. The award represents the conceptual ancestor of the typed task envelope: it fixes which agent owns the subtask and what structured receipt must return, eliminating the ambiguity inherent in prompt-level delegation.\n\n: **Blackboard Architecture**: The Hearsay-II speech-understanding system of the 1970s coordinated independent knowledge sources that never communicated directly with one another, interacting solely by posting and reading hypotheses on a shared global data structure. Its central scheduling challenge, deciding which pending contribution to evaluate next, reappears whenever agent workers claim tasks from a shared operational store.\n\nThose structural differences directly govern communication cost, because message counts scale with the communication graph and its interaction protocol. If each of $\\WorkerCount$ participants transmits an update to every other participant during an execution round, the network must handle $\\WorkerCount(\\WorkerCount-1)$ directed deliveries. With an average payload size of $\\MeanDeliveryPayloadLength$ tokens per delivery, that single round consumes $\\WorkerCount(\\WorkerCount-1)\\MeanDeliveryPayloadLength$ tokens across the system. In contrast, a supervisor managing $\\WorkerCount$ workers requires only $2\\WorkerCount$ deliveries per round (one dispatch envelope and one receipt per worker), consuming $2\\WorkerCount{}\\MeanDeliveryPayloadLength$ tokens. The full mesh thus scales quadratically, transmitting $(\\WorkerCount-1)/2$ times more messages per round than the centralized coordinator.\n\nRaw transmission volume measures network traffic, not what the receiving models must ingest. In a fully connected mesh where each agent appends every incoming peer message to its prompt history, ingested tokens per round grow as $\\mathcal{O}(\\WorkerCount^2)$. The append-only re-send of @eq-vol3-trajectory-resend compounds that growth across rounds, because each later prompt carries every earlier delivery. Even in a centralized topology, verbose receipts, iterative deliberation rounds, or frequent status polling against a blackboard store can exhaust context windows and inflate inference budgets. Across the four arrangements of @fig-vol3-multi-agent-topologies, the supervisor&#x27;s round costs $2\\WorkerCount$ deliveries and the actor mesh up to $\\WorkerCount(\\WorkerCount-1)$, while the pipeline and blackboard replace direct deliveries with bounded queues and versioned claims that the runtime must police. An arrangement therefore keeps context windows bounded only when each handoff carries the specific inputs and checks a subtask requires, and deciding when those handoffs may fire belongs to the dependency graph.\n\n::: {#fig-vol3-multi-agent-topologies fig-env=&quot;figure&quot; fig-pos=&quot;htb&quot; fig-cap=&quot;**Four Coordination Arrangements**: Each panel shows where work crosses a boundary. A supervisor sends envelopes down and collects receipts, a pipeline passes artifacts through bounded queues, a blackboard mediates claims and versioned publications through a shared store, and actors exchange addressed messages through mailboxes. One supervisor round costs $2\\WorkerCount$ deliveries and a full mesh round $\\WorkerCount(\\WorkerCount-1)$. Each topology imposes specific runtime obligations for bounding queues, isolating concurrent state, and verifying result correctness.&quot; fig-alt=&quot;Four panels. Panel a: a wide supervisor box above three worker boxes, each worker with a downward solid envelope arrow and an upward dashed receipt arrow, labeled 2N per round. Panel b: stage 1, stage 2, and stage 3 in a row, separated by two bounded queues drawn as three slots each, partly occupied, with artifacts passing through them. Panel c: three worker boxes sending claim and publish arrows to a shared store holding task 2 claimed and artifact versions 2 and 3, with dashed read arrows back. Panel d: four actor boxes, each with a small mailbox of slots, joined by faint dashed links of the possible full mesh, labeled N times N minus 1, with three solid addressed messages drawn on it.&quot;}\n\n:::\n\n### Dependency graphs and concurrency {#sec-vol3-multiagent-dependency-graphs}\n\nDispatching subtasks before their prerequisites are verified wastes tokens on stale assumptions or stalls worker slots behind unfinished upstream steps. To schedule concurrent work safely, the runtime models task precedence as a directed acyclic graph (DAG), $(\\DependencyGraphVertexSet,\\DependencyGraphEdgeSet)$. Each vertex $\\DependencyGraphVertex \\in \\DependencyGraphVertexSet$ represents a bounded unit of execution, either an autonomous agent trajectory or a deterministic runtime validation check. A directed edge $(\\PredecessorGraphVertex,\\DependencyGraphVertex) \\in \\DependencyGraphEdgeSet$ indicates that vertex $\\DependencyGraphVertex$ requires a declared precondition satisfied by vertex $\\PredecessorGraphVertex$, such as an emitted code artifact, a validated test report, or an explicit authorization token. A status message signaling task completion satisfies an incoming edge only when accompanied by verifiable output receipts.\n\nA vertex becomes **dependency-ready** once every prerequisite predecessor condition has been verified. Dependency readiness alone does not trigger execution, however. The runtime dispatcher must additionally verify execution authority, allocate an active worker slot, and secure a bounded token and financial budget reservation. If a predecessor fails or emits invalid artifacts, the control plane either halts downstream successors or transitions execution to an explicit recovery branch. Loops and iterative refinement cycles enter the graph as unrolled, bounded attempts. Because graph acyclicity guarantees only the absence of logical dependency loops, the underlying runtime remains responsible for detecting dynamic deadlocks arising from shared resource locks, exhausted thread pools, or saturated queues.\n\nTo estimate the theoretical limits of parallel scheduling, assume each vertex $\\DependencyGraphVertex$ occupies one interchangeable worker slot for a fixed duration $\\VertexSlotTime(\\DependencyGraphVertex)$, encompassing both model inference latency and external tool execution. Let $\\WorkerCount$ represent the pool of available parallel worker slots. Under these assumptions, aggregate slot-work $\\AggregateSlotWork$ totals every vertex&#x27;s duration, and critical-path duration $\\CriticalPathDuration$ is the length of the longest dependency chain, the same critical path that trace analysis measures from recorded spans, here computed from assumed durations:\n\n$$\n\\AggregateSlotWork=\\sum_{\\DependencyGraphVertex\\in \\DependencyGraphVertexSet}\\VertexSlotTime(\\DependencyGraphVertex),\\qquad\n\\CriticalPathDuration=\\max_{\\pi\\in\\operatorname{Paths}(\\DependencyGraphVertexSet,\\DependencyGraphEdgeSet)}\\sum_{\\DependencyGraphVertex\\in\\pi}\\VertexSlotTime(\\DependencyGraphVertex).\n$$ {#eq-vol3-multiagent-dag-work}\n\nAny valid schedule&#x27;s total makespan $\\ScheduleFinishTime$ must satisfy the dual lower bounds\n\n$$\n\\ScheduleFinishTime\\ge\\max\\left(\\frac{\\AggregateSlotWork}{\\WorkerCount},\\CriticalPathDuration\\right).\n$$ {#eq-vol3-multiagent-dag-bound}\n\nThe aggregate work bound $\\AggregateSlotWork/\\WorkerCount$ reflects the capacity constraint, since $\\WorkerCount$ parallel worker slots cannot complete $\\AggregateSlotWork$ total units of execution time in less than $\\AggregateSlotWork/\\WorkerCount$, regardless of schedule structure. The critical-path bound $\\CriticalPathDuration$ reflects the causal precedence constraint, since sequential dependencies cannot be parallelized, and it establishes a lower bound on latency even with infinite worker slots. In production environments, resource contention, queuing overhead, payload serialization, and trajectory retries expand the overall makespan well beyond these theoretical minimums unless explicitly incorporated into vertex durations $\\VertexSlotTime(\\DependencyGraphVertex)$ and graph edges.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ Trace-based measurement of the critical path is established in @sec-vol3-evaluation-tracing-latency.\n:::\n\n: **Greedy List Scheduling**: Graham established that any list schedule which never leaves a worker slot idle while a dependency-ready vertex waits completes within a factor of $2-1/\\WorkerCount$ of the optimal makespan. Graham also demonstrated that such schedules can suffer counterintuitive timing anomalies where adding worker slots or reducing individual task durations actually increases overall completion time [@graham1969bounds]. A runtime dispatcher that greedily schedules ready work is therefore near-optimal relative to the theoretical bound in @eq-vol3-multiagent-dag-bound, and an observed latency regression after expanding worker pools does not necessarily indicate a scheduler implementation bug.\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ DEPENDENCY-LIMITED SCHEDULE (LEGO)</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: @nbk-17-critical-path-makespan-and-dag-scheduling and its table.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal: Compare aggregate work with an attainable idealized DAG schedule.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show: Node durations, earliest starts/finishes, bounds, and speedup.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How: Assumed slot durations (Scenarios.InterfaceMigrationSchedule);</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">│      predecessor-max recurrence in topological order.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import *</p>\n<p>class DagSchedule:</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Illustrative assumptions) ─────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>plan = Scenarios.InterfaceMigrationSchedule workers = plan.slots duration = plan.durations predecessors = plan.predecessors</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Unlimited-slot earliest schedule; check capacity below) ─</h1>\n<p>start = plan.start finish = plan.finish work = plan.work critical = plan.makespan work_bound = plan.work_bound peak_slots = plan.peak_slots speedup = plan.speedup slack6 = plan.free_float[6]  # float on the only off-critical branch into v7</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Feasible capacity and the stated comparison) ──────────────</h1>\n<p>check(work == 275 <em> second, \"Aggregate work changed\") check(critical == 174.5 </em> second, \"Critical path changed\") check(peak_slots == 3 and peak_slots &lt;= workers, \"Schedule exceeds capacity\") check(work_bound &lt; critical, \"Work bound now dominates\") check(1.575 &lt; speedup &lt; 1.585, \"Displayed speedup changed\") check(slack6 == 26.5 * second, \"Slack on the v4-v6 branch changed\") check(finish[2] == finish[3], \"v2 and v3 must tie for the critical path\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ─────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>workers_str = fmt_int(workers) peak_slots_str = fmt_int(peak_slots) d1_str = fmt_time(duration[1], unit=second, precision=1) d2_str = fmt_time(duration[2], unit=second, precision=0) d5_str = fmt_time(duration[5], unit=second, precision=0) d6_str = fmt_time(duration[6], unit=second, precision=1) d7_str = fmt_time(duration[7], unit=second, precision=0) start1_str = fmt_time(start[1], unit=second, precision=0) start2_str = fmt_time(start[2], unit=second, precision=1) start5_str = fmt_time(start[5], unit=second, precision=1) start7_str = fmt_time(start[7], unit=second, precision=1) finish6_str = fmt_time(finish[6], unit=second, precision=0) work_str = fmt_time(work, unit=second, precision=0) critical_str = fmt_time(critical, unit=second, precision=1) work_bound_str = fmt_time(work_bound, unit=second, precision=2) speedup_mult_str = fmt_multiple(speedup, precision=2) slack6_str = fmt_time(slack6, unit=second, precision=1) ]```</p>\n<p><strong>Problem</strong>: Consider an illustrative repository upgrade with <code>[DagSchedule.workers_str]</code> worker slots. @Tbl-vol3-multiagent-dag-schedule gives assumed durations for a single successful execution of each node. Each node occupies one slot; there are no additional dispatch delays, resource conflicts, retries, or transfer costs. How much can overlap?</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Work</th><th>Required predecessors</th><th>Slot duration</th><th>Earliest start</th><th>Earliest finish</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>$\\DependencyGraphVertex_1$: prepare shared specification</td><td>None</td><td><code>[DagSchedule.d1_str]</code></td><td><code>[DagSchedule.start1_str]</code></td><td><code>[DagSchedule.start2_str]</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td>$\\DependencyGraphVertex_2,\\DependencyGraphVertex_3,\\DependencyGraphVertex_4$: refactor separate modules</td><td>$\\DependencyGraphVertex_1$, for each</td><td><code>[DagSchedule.d2_str]</code> each</td><td><code>[DagSchedule.start2_str]</code></td><td><code>[DagSchedule.start5_str]</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td>$\\DependencyGraphVertex_5$: assess the joint changes from two modules</td><td>$\\DependencyGraphVertex_2,\\DependencyGraphVertex_3$</td><td><code>[DagSchedule.d5_str]</code></td><td><code>[DagSchedule.start5_str]</code></td><td><code>[DagSchedule.start7_str]</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td>$\\DependencyGraphVertex_6$: prepare bindings for the remaining module</td><td>$\\DependencyGraphVertex_4$</td><td><code>[DagSchedule.d6_str]</code></td><td><code>[DagSchedule.start5_str]</code></td><td><code>[DagSchedule.finish6_str]</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td>$\\DependencyGraphVertex_7$: integrate and run required regression checks</td><td>$\\DependencyGraphVertex_5,\\DependencyGraphVertex_6$</td><td><code>[DagSchedule.d7_str]</code></td><td><code>[DagSchedule.start7_str]</code></td><td><code>[DagSchedule.critical_str]</code></td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>An idealized dependency schedule</strong>: Starts are the latest finish among required predecessors. The schedule assumes that each predecessor supplies the result required by its successor. {#tbl-vol3-multiagent-dag-schedule}</p>\n<p><strong>Math</strong>: Summing the node durations as in @eq-vol3-multiagent-dag-work gives <code>[DagSchedule.work_str]</code> of aggregate slot-work. Dividing by the slot count gives the work term in @eq-vol3-multiagent-dag-bound, <code>[DagSchedule.work_bound_str]</code>. The paths $\\DependencyGraphVertex_1\\to \\DependencyGraphVertex_2\\to \\DependencyGraphVertex_5\\to \\DependencyGraphVertex_7$ and $\\DependencyGraphVertex_1\\to \\DependencyGraphVertex_3\\to \\DependencyGraphVertex_5\\to \\DependencyGraphVertex_7$ tie for the critical path. The table's schedule uses at most <code>[DagSchedule.peak_slots_str]</code> slots, so it is feasible under the stated capacity and attains the path bound.</p>\n<p><strong>Result</strong>: Completion takes <code>[DagSchedule.critical_str]</code>, a <code>[DagSchedule.speedup_mult_str]</code> speedup over executing these same nodes serially, and more slots cannot shorten the remaining chain.</p>\n<p><strong>Systems insight</strong>: Once a schedule attains the critical-path bound, as this one does, allocating worker slots beyond peak concurrency yields zero latency improvement. The off-critical branch through $\\DependencyGraphVertex_4$ and $\\DependencyGraphVertex_6$ finishes <code>[DagSchedule.slack6_str]</code> before $\\DependencyGraphVertex_7$ can start, meaning optimizations on that branch do not affect overall makespan. In contrast, shortening $\\DependencyGraphVertex_5$ or $\\DependencyGraphVertex_7$ yields a direct second-for-second reduction in total makespan up to the available slack. Shortening $\\DependencyGraphVertex_2$ in isolation provides no benefit because $\\DependencyGraphVertex_3$ ties it on the critical path. Analyzing production agent traces requires distinguishing a dependency-limited schedule like this one from a capacity-limited schedule, where additional slots or reduced contention can still shorten makespan.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h3\">Static plans and dynamic spawning</h3>\n<p>When a coordinator fixes every subtask before execution begins, workers cannot branch when they uncover an undocumented dependency. Conversely, letting a model spawn children at will can turn a simple query into dozens of redundant trajectories. A <strong>static plan</strong> resolves this trade-off toward predictability by specifying its stages, conditional branches, validation checkpoints, and retry bounds ahead of execution. Because all artifact dependencies and message routes are declared upfront, operators can inspect handoffs statically and verify safety invariants before dispatch. With enforced per-node work limits, bounded attempts, and stated service or deadline assumptions, the declared graph supports worst-case resource and execution bounds. A termination deadline does not guarantee accepted completion or immediate settlement of external work.</p>\n<p>In contrast, <strong>dynamic spawning</strong> provisions child agents at runtime in response to intermediate observations. For example, during a repository migration, a worker analyzing call sites might uncover an undocumented dependency and request an isolated sub-investigation. While the model proposes this subtask dynamically, the runtime control plane must evaluate and admit it under the prevailing task contract. Dynamic spawning allows systems to adapt flexibly to open-ended workloads, but it introduces non-deterministic execution graphs. When an unconstrained model decides how many child agents to spawn and when to spawn them, combinatorial fan-out can quickly overwhelm system capacity and inflate execution costs.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ SOURCED FIGURES FROM A MULTI-AGENT RESEARCH SYSTEM REPORT (LEGO)\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: war story \\ref{ws-vol3-multiagent-research-fanout-2025} and\n# │          notebook \\ref{nbk-17-token-multiplier-break-even}.\n# │ Goal: Hold the report&#x27;s published figures in one place.\n# │ Show: Early fan-out, token multiples over chat, and the evaluation gain.\n# │ How: Constants quoted from @anthropic2025multiagent; no derivation here.\n# │      The two token multiples live on the Agents.Research.OrchestratorWorker\n# │      profile, whose provenance cites the report; the fan-out count and the\n# │      evaluation gain are cited literature entries.\n# │ Note: Reported by the system&#x27;s builders on their own internal evaluation.\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import Agents, Literature\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import check, fmt_int, fmt_multiple, fmt_percent\n\nclass ResearchReport:\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Quoted from anthropic2025multiagent) ────────────────────\n    research = Agents.Research.OrchestratorWorker\n    early_subagents = 50  # subagents spawned for simple queries in early versions\n    single_agent_tokens_vs_chat = research.agent_token_multiple  # &quot;about 4x more tokens than chat&quot;\n    multi_agent_tokens_vs_chat = research.fanout_token_multiple  # &quot;about 15x more tokens than chats&quot;\n    eval_gain = 90.2 / 100  # multi-agent over single-agent, internal research eval\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (None: quoted values) ─────────────────────────────────\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Transcription sanity) ─────────────────────────────────\n    check(multi_agent_tokens_vs_chat &gt; single_agent_tokens_vs_chat &gt; 1, &quot;Token multiples must be ordered&quot;)\n    check(0 &lt; eval_gain &lt; 1, &quot;Relative gain transcribed as a fraction&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────\n    early_subagents_str = fmt_int(early_subagents)\n    single_vs_chat_mult_str = fmt_multiple(single_agent_tokens_vs_chat, precision=0)\n    multi_vs_chat_mult_str = fmt_multiple(multi_agent_tokens_vs_chat, precision=0)\n    eval_gain_str = fmt_percent(eval_gain, precision=1, style=&quot;prose&quot;)\n]```\n\n::: {#ws-vol3-multiagent-research-fanout-2025 .callout-war-story title=&quot;Unbounded fan-out in a multi-agent research system (2025)&quot;}\n\n**Context**: Anthropic&#x27;s engineering team described building an autonomous research system where a lead orchestrator plans an investigation, spawns parallel subagents to search for evidence, and synthesizes their findings [@anthropic2025multiagent].\n\n**Mechanism**: The lead agent possessed unconstrained authority to determine subagent fan-out. In early prototype versions, the model routinely spawned `[ResearchReport.early_subagents_str]` subagents even for simple queries, searched endlessly for nonexistent sources, and allowed subagents to degrade coordination by flooding one another with excessive updates.\n\n**Impact**: The engineering team reports that autonomous agents typically consume about `[ResearchReport.single_vs_chat_mult_str]` more tokens than standard conversational interactions, while multi-agent systems consume roughly `[ResearchReport.multi_vs_chat_mult_str]` more. Although the multi-agent configuration outperformed a single agent by `[ResearchReport.eval_gain_str]` on the team&#x27;s internal research evaluation, the unconstrained fan-out incurred disproportionate resource expenditure on straightforward tasks.\n\n**Fix**: The team embedded explicit scaling heuristics into the orchestrator&#x27;s prompt, instructing the model to calibrate subagent allocation to query complexity, allocating a single agent for direct fact retrieval and scaling upward only for open-ended synthesis. The orchestrator was also structured to wait synchronously for each dispatched subagent batch to conclude before initiating subsequent steps, simplifying state synchronization at the cost of pipeline parallelism.\n\n**Systems lesson**: Dynamic fan-out is an architectural resource-allocation problem that cannot rely on model self-restraint alone. While prompt-based heuristics guide model proposals, the runtime control plane must enforce hard admission limits on tree breadth, recursion depth, and aggregate budget reservations. These limits directly bound the token consumption in the effective cost metric $\\CostPerAcceptedTask$.\n\n:::\n\nUncontrolled fan-out illustrates the necessity of formal admission control in multi-agent architectures. Prompt instructions alone cannot curb subtask generation, because an unconstrained model can still propose redundant branches or trigger cascading worker requests. Allocating each child a budget fraction smaller than the parent&#x27;s remaining funds also fails to protect the system, as a burst of concurrent siblings can issue overlapping claims that collectively exceed available capacity. The runtime admission controller must atomically reserve each child&#x27;s maximum potential liability against the shared task budget before dispatching execution, an invariant that @sec-vol3-multiagent-authority formalizes across hierarchical delegation trees. Production runtimes must independently enforce four distinct bounds: fan-out breadth (maximum children per parent), concurrent active slots (maximum parallel executions), recursion depth (maximum delegation layers), and absolute deadlines. A shallow tree can still exhaust resources through excessive breadth, while a strict concurrency cap can still permit an unbounded sequence of sequential worker allocations.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ Bounded-liability admission, which reserves an operation&#x27;s maximum charge before admitting it, is established in @sec-vol3-agent-harness-reservation-settlement.\n:::\n\n### Supervision trees {#sec-vol3-multiagent-supervision-trees}\n\nBeyond resource management, dispatching a child agent establishes lifecycle ownership. A child&#x27;s running trajectory, pending tool invocations, and uncommitted side effects typically outlive the ephemeral model call that spawned it. The supervision tree defines how the runtime isolates faults and manages recovery when a child encounters an unrecoverable failure. Following the supervision trees of Erlang/OTP (Open Telecom Platform), systems formalize recovery through explicit, per-supervisor restart strategies [@armstrong2003making; @armstrong2007erlang]:\n\n- Under a `one_for_one` policy, the supervisor restarts only the failed child agent while allowing sibling workers to continue uninterrupted. This strategy is ideal for embarrassingly parallel or loosely coupled subtasks, such as searching disjoint documentation sources, where workers share no mutable intermediate state.\n- Under a `one_for_all` policy, the failure of any single child triggers the supervisor to terminate and restart all sibling workers within that supervision group. This approach is needed for tightly coupled collaborative tasks where partial artifacts become invalid upon peer failure. For example, if an interface worker crashes and restarts with an altered API contract, any partial implementation generated by a concurrent caller worker is invalidated.\n\n: **Erlang Supervision Trees**: Erlang emerged from a telephone-switching research program initiated in 1981, established on the design principle that systems should allow failing processes to crash cleanly while a designated supervisor restores known-good state, rather than attempting defensive error handling locally [@armstrong2003making]. In agentic systems, this principle is even more vital: allowing a confused agent trajectory to attempt local recovery within a contaminated context window frequently compounds its errors, whereas a supervisor restart provisions a clean execution context from recorded inputs.\n\nThe two policies differ in blast radius (@fig-vol3-supervisor-tree-isolation): one failure under `one_for_one` costs a single fresh attempt and its reservation, whereas under `one_for_all` it discards the progress of every coupled sibling and restarts the whole group from recorded inputs. To prevent degenerate retry storms under either policy, supervision trees enforce a **restart intensity** bound, the maximum number of restarts permitted within a sliding time window. When a child agent repeatedly fails and breaches this threshold, the supervisor ceases local restarts, aborts its remaining subordinates, and escalates the failure upward to its own parent supervisor. In an agentic system, a restart must never continue from the failed worker&#x27;s corrupted or circular context window. Instead, the runtime treats each restart as a freshly admitted attempt. It provisions a clean context initialized from recorded input artifacts and acquires a fresh budget reservation. Whenever it aborts sibling processes under `one_for_all` or escalation, it also invokes the structured cancellation and cleanup protocols of @sec-vol3-multiagent-backpressure.\n\n::: {#fig-vol3-supervisor-tree-isolation fig-env=&quot;figure&quot; fig-pos=&quot;htb&quot; fig-cap=&quot;**Restart Policies in a Supervision Tree**: Under the one-for-one policy (a), only the failed worker restarts, as a newly admitted attempt with its own reservation, while its siblings continue. Under the one-for-all policy (b), the supervisor stops the coupled siblings through the cancellation protocol and restarts the whole group from recorded inputs, because the caller&#x27;s partial work has lost its meaning. Either supervisor that exceeds its declared restart intensity stops restarting and escalates the failure to its parent.&quot; fig-alt=&quot;A parent supervisor box above two cards. Card a, one_for_one: a supervisor with three worker children; the middle worker is marked failed and is the only node inside a dashed red blast radius, with an arrow labeled restart to a new attempt box with its own reservation, while the other two workers are marked continues. Card b, one_for_all: a supervisor with interface, caller, and test children all inside one dashed red blast radius; caller is marked failed, interface and test are marked stopped, and arrows from all three lead to one box labeled group restarts, new attempts from recorded inputs. A dashed red arrow from supervisor b to the parent is labeled restart intensity exceeded, escalate.&quot;}\n\n:::\n\n::: {#chk-17-amdahl-concurrency .callout-checkpoint title=&quot;Evaluating multi-agent coordination topologies&quot;}\n\n- [ ] **Dependency vs. routing**: The interface and caller workers communicate only through their supervisor. Why can they still produce interdependent patches, and which of the three structures records that?\n- [ ] **Critical-path estimate**: Using the durations in @tbl-vol3-multiagent-dag-schedule, estimate how much completion changes if $\\DependencyGraphVertex_6$ is removed entirely, and how much if $\\DependencyGraphVertex_5$ takes 20 s less. Which node should an engineering team speed up?\n- [ ] **Worker-count screen**: With $\\ParallelFraction=0.9$ and the notebook&#x27;s overhead $\\WorkerOverheadSlope=$ `[DelegationScreen.overhead_str]` per extra worker, estimate the best worker count from $\\sqrt{\\ParallelFraction/\\WorkerOverheadSlope}$ and the speedup ceiling $1/(1-\\ParallelFraction)$. Is it worth halving $\\WorkerOverheadSlope$ or halving $1-\\ParallelFraction$?\n- [ ] **Aggregate admission**: Why can granting every child less than its parent&#x27;s remaining budget still oversubscribe the task? What must happen atomically?\n:::\n\nA coordination topology fixes how work, messages, and lifecycle ownership cross agent boundaries, but routing and supervision alone do not specify what a child is allowed to do once it starts. Without an explicit contract on each handoff, a worker can still read stale inputs, touch files outside its scope, or return a long narrative in place of verifiable evidence. Every boundary crossing therefore requires a formal task envelope that states what artifacts the worker receives, what tools and tokens it may consume, and what typed receipt it must return.\n\n## Typed Task Envelopes {#sec-vol3-multiagent-contracts}\n\nA message asking `worker_caller_01` to &quot;update the callers&quot; leaves critical engineering boundaries unspecified: the target interface version, the files permitted to change, the resource budget, and the acceptance criteria for completion. If the worker fills those gaps from its own interpretation of the prompt, that interpretation becomes the de facto contract. While a workflow dependency edge dictates when a child task may begin, the task envelope defines what that child is permitted to read, modify, spend, and return. Delegation therefore requires a typed action contract (principle \\ref{pri-vol3-strict-action-abi}) that extends across the agent boundary. It works much like a typed interface in a classic remote procedure call, which establishes formal argument types, return values, and failure semantics before execution begins [@birrell1984rpc].\n\n::: {#dfn-typed-task-envelope .callout-definition title=&quot;Typed task envelope&quot;}\n\nA ***typed task envelope*** is a versioned assignment record binding a child task to identified inputs, a scoped authority grant, resource and lifecycle limits, completion requirements, and a bounded return format.\n\n1. **Significance**: The runtime validates task boundaries before admitting work and verifies returned artifacts against required evidence. In addition, capping the return payload prevents runaway worker outputs from flooding the coordinator&#x27;s context window on subsequent execution turns.\n2. **Distinction**: The envelope defines an operational, enforceable contract for execution and resources; the natural-language prompt merely describes the objective within those constraints.\n3. **Common pitfall**: Mistaking a syntactically valid envelope for proof of authorization or successful execution. Both authorization and task completion require verification against authoritative state records maintained outside the worker&#x27;s control.\n\n:::\n\n### Task assignments {#sec-vol3-multiagent-task-assignments}\n\nIf a parent passes a subtask in free-form prose or embeds mutable budget numbers directly inside the child&#x27;s prompt, the child can misread its scope or rewrite its own limits. When delegating a subtask, the parent agent proposes the work, but the execution runtime authenticates the parent-child relationship, resolves the authority grant, and locks the resource reservation. In the task envelope shown in @lst-vol3-task-envelope, field identifiers point to immutable registry records rather than embedding raw, unverified data. The referenced acceptance contract specifies expected caller behavior, exact test suite versions, the execution environment, and any required human reviews prior to final acceptance.\n\n::: {#lst-vol3-task-envelope lst-cap=&quot;**Bounded Child Assignment**: The envelope identifies inputs, authority, resource ownership, acceptance requirements, and limits on the return.&quot;}\n</code></pre>\n<p>{ \"task_id\": \"worker_caller_01\", \"parent_task_id\": \"migration_task_01\", \"envelope_version\": \"caller_assignment_v1\", \"dispatch_id\": \"caller_dispatch_01\", \"assignment\": \"Update the designated authentication callers.\", \"inputs\": {\"base_ref\": \"repo_base_01\"}, \"authority\": {\"grant_ref\": \"caller_private_patch_grant_01\"}, \"budget\": {\"reservation_ref\": \"caller_reservation_01\"}, \"completion\": { \"contract_ref\": \"migration_accept_v1\", \"required_evidence\": [ \"candidate_ref\", \"write_set_ref\", \"dependencies_ref\", \"check_report_ref\", \"usage_ref\", \"settlement_ref\" ] }, \"return_limits\": { \"receipt_bytes\": 8192, \"optional_summary_tokens\": 256, \"artifact_references\": 8 } }</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>\n:::\n\nIn @lst-vol3-task-envelope, the task and dispatch identifiers tie the assignment to its durable write-ahead log intent, so that recovery can reconcile unresolved work before any permitted retry. The field `base_ref` pins the immutable repository snapshot exposed to the worker (any reads beyond that snapshot, such as queries to external package registries, require their own versioned references). Within that snapshot, `grant_ref` restricts the worker to editing designated caller files in an isolated scratch workspace, reading approved paths, and running local verification commands. The capability gate enforces parameter-level constraints on each tool invocation and explicitly withholds publication rights to shared branches. Alongside that authority grant, `reservation_ref` establishes hard limits on consumable resources, including input and output tokens as well as tool execution counts. Because the runtime resolves all three references against authoritative registry state before dispatch, a worker cannot expand its privileges or budget by modifying envelope fields in its own context.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ Durable intent, the admitted request recorded before dispatch so recovery retains its identity and can resolve an uncertain outcome, is established in @sec-vol3-persistence-wal.\n:::\n\nThe remaining two blocks in @lst-vol3-task-envelope govern how the child returns its work. Completion requirements name the evidence records the runtime must collect, such as candidate diffs, dependency manifests, check reports, and usage and settlement records, before the task can count as complete. Return limits cap the receipt size, optional summary tokens, and artifact references so bulky build outputs stay in external storage rather than flooding the coordinator&#x27;s context window. The receipt described next records how the child met those rules.\n\n### Task receipts {#sec-vol3-multiagent-task-receipts}\n\nWhen the child finishes or halts within those limits, the runtime packages the result into a receipt that mirrors the assignment envelope. The return payload in @lst-vol3-task-receipt describes a candidate change ready for integration review, one step short of an accepted modification. It strictly separates verified operational metadata from unverified model commentary. Runtime services directly populate and authenticate every field under `runtime`, whereas assertions generated by the worker model remain isolated under `worker_claims`.\n\n::: {#lst-vol3-task-receipt lst-cap=&quot;**Bounded Child Receipt**: Runtime records and a worker-authored explanation remain distinct; candidate readiness does not authorize publication.&quot;}\n</code></pre>\n<p>{ \"task_id\": \"worker_caller_01\", \"envelope_version\": \"caller_assignment_v1\", \"dispatch_id\": \"caller_dispatch_01\", \"return_status\": \"complete\", \"runtime\": { \"status\": \"candidate_ready\", \"base_ref\": \"repo_base_01\", \"candidate_ref\": \"caller_candidate_01\", \"write_set_ref\": \"caller_writes_01\", \"dependencies_ref\": \"caller_dependencies_01\", \"check_report_ref\": \"caller_checks_01\", \"usage_ref\": \"caller_usage_01\", \"settlement_ref\": \"caller_settlement_01\" }, \"worker_claims\": { \"summary\": \"Updated the designated callers for the migration.\" } }</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>\n:::\n\nThe write-set record reflects the diff actually produced in the isolated workspace rather than the worker&#x27;s conversational recollection of what it modified. Similarly, the dependency record captures observed file reads, loaded package versions, configuration files, and external environment flags, bounded by explicit tracing coverage. The checker report records the exact candidate hash, the test command, the container environment, and the exit status. This record carries the evidence-scope rule of the tool boundary across the delegation boundary. A single call&#x27;s observation confirmed only what that call measured. A check result here establishes an outcome for one identified candidate hash, checker, and environment, and authorizes nothing beyond it. If the runtime cannot produce or verify every piece of mandatory evidence, the return status transitions to `evidence_unavailable` rather than `complete`.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ The evidence-scope rule, under which an observation confirms only what its tool measured during that invocation, is established in @sec-vol3-tool-calling-complete-mediation.\n:::\n\nThe usage record accounts for charged and reserved work across tokens, wall-clock time, and tool invocations. The settlement record distinguishes a stop acknowledgment, confirmed resource release, and unresolved background operations with retained owners. A `complete` return and the final settlement of every operation are therefore distinct statuses. This separation matters when the parent cancels an execution branch (@sec-vol3-multiagent-backpressure), ensuring that task termination does not leave orphaned background processes or dangling locks. Optional prose explanations may be shortened or omitted when space is constrained, with the omission explicitly recorded, while required identities, statuses, and evidence references pass intact. If the mandatory structural fields alone exceed `receipt_bytes`, the runtime generates an explicit payload-size failure rather than returning a truncated record.\n\n### Reference passing and selective reads {#sec-vol3-multiagent-reference-passing}\n\nJust as receipts pass artifact references to stay within payload limits, a parent assignment passes an immutable repository reference (`base_ref`) instead of inlining an entire repository tree into a child task&#x27;s context prompt. The worker then issues targeted read queries to fetch only the source files relevant to its assignment. However, this pass-by-reference pattern yields net token savings only when the child inspects a small fraction of the code base. If the worker iteratively fetches broad directory trees, the combined cost of reference overhead, tool invocation tokens, and repeated context re-reads across turns can quickly outstrip the cost of an upfront payload. Passing a shared base reference also amortizes KV-cache prefill across parallel workers, but only when their prompt prefixes align.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ Prompt prefix alignment and KV-cache prefill amortization are established in @sec-vol3-context-engineering-exact-prefix.\n:::\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| output: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-7\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-7\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ REFERENCE-PASSING INPUT COST (LEGO)</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-7\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: @nbk-vol3-multiagent-reference-reads below.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-7\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal: Count repository input after the child follows a reference.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-7\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show: Pasted, selectively retrieved, and fully retrieved input totals.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-7\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How: Each supplied token is charged once at an illustrative input price.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-7\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import Scenarios, check, fmt_int, fmt_multiple, fmt_percent, fmt_usd, USD</p>\n<p>class RefPassing:</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-7\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Hypothetical repository-delivery inputs) ─────────────────</h1>\n<p>handoff = Scenarios.SixChildReferencePassing workers = handoff.workers slice_tokens = handoff.slice_tokens envelope_ref_tokens = handoff.reference_tokens files_read_tokens = handoff.read_tokens input_price_per_mtok = handoff.input_price.to(\"USD / Mtoken\").magnitude * USD</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-7\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Charge each supplied token once) ──────────────────────</h1>\n<p>pasted_tokens = handoff.pasted_tokens ref_tokens = handoff.reference_total_tokens full_read_tokens = handoff.full_read_tokens pasted_usd = handoff.pasted_cost ref_usd = handoff.reference_cost ratio = handoff.reduction read_fraction = handoff.read_fraction breakeven_read_tokens = handoff.break_even_read_tokens  # reads at which the saving vanishes</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-7\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Selective and full-retrieval ordering) ──────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(0 &lt; ref_tokens &lt; pasted_tokens &lt; full_read_tokens, \"Input ordering\") check(full_read_tokens - pasted_tokens == workers * envelope_ref_tokens, \"Full retrieval retains the envelope overhead\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-7\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ───────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>workers_str = fmt_int(workers) slice_str = fmt_int(slice_tokens) envelope_ref_str = fmt_int(envelope_ref_tokens) files_read_str = fmt_int(files_read_tokens) input_price_str = fmt_usd(input_price_per_mtok, precision=0) pasted_str = fmt_int(pasted_tokens) ref_str = fmt_int(ref_tokens) full_read_str = fmt_int(full_read_tokens) pasted_usd_str = fmt_usd(pasted_usd, precision=3) ref_usd_str = fmt_usd(ref_usd, precision=3) ratio_mult_str = fmt_multiple(ratio, precision=1) read_fraction_str = fmt_percent(read_fraction, precision=1, style=\"prose\") breakeven_read_str = fmt_int(breakeven_read_tokens) ]```</p>\n<p><strong>Problem</strong>: Each of <code>[RefPassing.workers_str]</code> children receives either a <code>[RefPassing.slice_str]</code>-token repository slice or a <code>[RefPassing.envelope_ref_str]</code>-token reference envelope followed by <code>[RefPassing.files_read_str]</code> tokens of retrieved content. With all other inputs equal, no cache discount, and a hypothetical <code>[RefPassing.input_price_str]</code> per million input tokens, how much repository-delivery input does the reference save?</p>\n<p><strong>Math</strong>: Pasting costs <code>[RefPassing.workers_str]</code> $\\times$ <code>[RefPassing.slice_str]</code> $=$ <code>[RefPassing.pasted_str]</code> tokens, or <code>[RefPassing.pasted_usd_str]</code>. The reference costs <code>[RefPassing.workers_str]</code> $\\times$ (<code>[RefPassing.envelope_ref_str]</code> $+$ <code>[RefPassing.files_read_str]</code>) $=$ <code>[RefPassing.ref_str]</code> tokens, or <code>[RefPassing.ref_usd_str]</code>, a <code>[RefPassing.ratio_mult_str]</code> reduction. If every child instead reads the full slice, the reference design consumes <code>[RefPassing.full_read_str]</code> tokens and loses its saving.</p>\n<p><strong>Systems insight</strong>: The efficiency of reference passing depends entirely on read selectivity rather than the pointer mechanism alone. In this configuration, each child inspects only <code>[RefPassing.read_fraction_str]</code> of the slice, keeping reference passing economical until reads exceed <code>[RefPassing.breakeven_read_str]</code> tokens. Effective multi-agent system design therefore pairs pointer handoffs with precise task scoping and indexing.</p>\n<p>Once execution concludes, the receipt hands the parent coordinator an isolated candidate, its base version, and runtime-recorded evidence. Those records, not the absence of overlapping files, decide whether candidates can merge safely, because two patches that touch disjoint files can still break each other when integrated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-7\" class=\"cs-h2\">Optimistic Concurrency Control</h2>\n<p>In <code>migration_task_01</code>, both <code>worker_interface_01</code> and <code>worker_caller_01</code> branch from the same baseline commit, <code>repo_base_01</code>, to edit their respective files. When the interface worker finishes first, the integration service accepts its patch as <code>repo_head_02</code>. Because the caller worker modifies a different file, a standard three-way textual merge succeeds cleanly without merge conflicts. The caller's patch, however, still targets the obsolete interface. Modifying separate files prevented a textual collision, but it left the semantic dependency behind the caller's code unchecked.</p>\n<p><strong>Optimistic concurrency control (OCC)</strong> resolves this tension by letting each agent work privately against a snapshot and validating every publication prerequisite before the shared branch changes [@kung1981optimistic]. A transaction runs a read phase against private copies, a validation phase that checks whether concurrent commits invalidated anything it read, and a write phase that commits only after validation succeeds. The expected-version check at the tool boundary is that validation applied to a single file. The scheme pays when conflicts are rare, because it trades lock waiting for occasional discarded work. A worker's read phase, however, spans minutes of model generation and tool execution rather than the brief life of a database transaction, so a failed validation discards real inference spend. Its prerequisites include dependency freshness and acceptance evidence as well as unchanged files. Instead of holding a repository lock while an agent deliberates, the runtime decouples private execution from integration, which exposes parallelism and converts conflicts into the coordination tax of candidate revalidation and occasional discarded work.</p>\n<p>↩ Conditional updates that commit only while the target still matches its expected version are established in @sec-vol3-tool-calling-conditional-updates.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-8\" class=\"cs-h3\">Shared workspace hazards</h3>\n<p>Without that private-snapshot separation, operating multiple agents within a single shared working directory introduces three immediate failure modes. A <strong>lost update</strong> occurs when two workers inspect the same file, deliberate over multiple inference turns, and serialize their writes back to disk. The later write silently replaces the earlier contribution, and the operating system reports no error because both writes were valid. A <strong>torn dependency read</strong> emerges when an agent executes tests while a peer is actively modifying related files, for example by evaluating code after a new interface has landed but before the caller has been updated. The test runner executes against an incoherent intermediate state that no agent ever intended to propose. Finally, <em>tool-lock contention</em> strikes when concurrent build tools or version-control commands collide on local metadata locks, such as Git's <code>.git/index.lock</code>. One worker crashes on a locked index even when the underlying edits target entirely disjoint directories.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ WRITE COLLISIONS IN A SHARED WORKSPACE (LEGO)\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: @eq-vol3-multiagent-write-collision and the prose that follows it\n# │          in @sec-vol3-multiagent-concurrency.\n# │ Goal: Show that uniform, independent write sets collide at small fleet sizes.\n# │ Show: Collision probability for two and eight workers, and the smallest\n# │       fleet for which a collision is more likely than not.\n# │ How: Exact sequential product of disjoint-draw probabilities, checked\n# │      against the exponential (birthday) approximation\n# │      (Scenarios.ThousandFileSharedWrites).\n# │ Note: Hypothetical repository and write-set sizes, not measured workloads.\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import Scenarios\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import check, fmt_int, fmt_percent\n\nclass WriteCollision:\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Hypothetical repository and write sets) ─────────────────\n    workspace = Scenarios.ThousandFileSharedWrites\n    files = workspace.files\n    writes_per_worker = workspace.writes_per_worker\n    small_fleet = workspace.small_fleet\n    large_fleet = workspace.large_fleet\n    max_fleet = workspace.max_fleet\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (Probability that N write sets are pairwise disjoint) ─\n    no_collision = workspace.disjoint\n    approx_no_collision = workspace.disjoint_approx\n    small_collision = workspace.collision[small_fleet]\n    large_collision = workspace.collision[large_fleet]\n    even_odds_fleet = workspace.even_odds_fleet\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Prose claims) ─────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(abs(no_collision[1] - 1) &lt; 1e-12, &quot;One worker cannot collide&quot;)\n    check(abs(no_collision[large_fleet] - approx_no_collision[large_fleet]) &lt; 0.01, &quot;Approximation must track the exact product here&quot;)\n    check(0.09 &lt; small_collision &lt; 0.11, &quot;Two workers collide about one time in ten&quot;)\n    check(large_collision &gt; 0.9, &quot;Eight workers collide with high probability&quot;)\n    check(even_odds_fleet == 5, &quot;Five workers first exceed even odds&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────\n    files_str = fmt_int(files)\n    writes_str = fmt_int(writes_per_worker)\n    small_fleet_str = fmt_int(small_fleet)\n    large_fleet_str = fmt_int(large_fleet)\n    even_odds_fleet_str = fmt_int(even_odds_fleet)\n    small_collision_str = fmt_percent(small_collision, precision=1, style=&quot;prose&quot;)\n    small_disjoint_str = fmt_percent(1 - small_collision, precision=1, style=&quot;prose&quot;)\n    large_collision_str = fmt_percent(large_collision, precision=1, style=&quot;prose&quot;)\n]```\n\n\n\nThese collisions manifest surprisingly early as agent concurrency grows. Suppose a repository contains $\\RepositoryFileCount$ files and each of $\\WorkerCount$ workers modifies $\\WorkerWrittenFileCount$ distinct files, chosen uniformly and independently at random. Worker $\\EarlierWorkerCount+1$ avoids overlapping with all preceding workers only if it selects its $\\WorkerWrittenFileCount$ files exclusively from the $\\RepositoryFileCount-\\EarlierWorkerCount \\WorkerWrittenFileCount$ files that earlier workers left untouched. The probability that all $\\WorkerCount$ write sets remain pairwise disjoint is therefore\n\n$$\n\\Pr(\\text{no shared write})=\\prod_{k=1}^{\\WorkerCount-1}\\frac{\\binom{\\RepositoryFileCount-\\EarlierWorkerCount \\WorkerWrittenFileCount}{\\WorkerWrittenFileCount}}{\\binom{\\RepositoryFileCount}{\\WorkerWrittenFileCount}}\\approx\\exp\\left(-\\frac{\\WorkerCount(\\WorkerCount-1)\\WorkerWrittenFileCount^2}{2\\RepositoryFileCount}\\right).\n$$ {#eq-vol3-multiagent-write-collision}\n\n@Eq-vol3-multiagent-write-collision is the birthday bound generalized to multi-file write sets. When $\\WorkerWrittenFileCount$ is small relative to $\\RepositoryFileCount$, each of the $\\WorkerCount(\\WorkerCount-1)/2$ worker pairs compares $\\WorkerWrittenFileCount^2$ file choices against the $\\RepositoryFileCount$ repository files, so the exponent grows quadratically with both the worker count $\\WorkerCount$ and the write-set size $\\WorkerWrittenFileCount$. Because the exponent scales with the number of worker pairs rather than linearly with the worker count, collision odds mount rapidly. In a hypothetical repository of `[WriteCollision.files_str]` files where each worker touches `[WriteCollision.writes_str]` files, even `[WriteCollision.small_fleet_str]` concurrent workers collide with probability `[WriteCollision.small_collision_str]`. At `[WriteCollision.even_odds_fleet_str]` workers, a write collision becomes more likely than not, and scaling to `[WriteCollision.large_fleet_str]` workers drives the collision probability to `[WriteCollision.large_collision_str]`. Uniform random access is an illustrative baseline that understates risk when writes cluster, because actual agent modifications concentrate around package manifests, central routing tables, and shared configuration files. Rigorous workspace isolation is therefore an architectural prerequisite for multi-agent coordination, rather than a performance tuning knob reserved for large worker pools.\n\n: **Birthday Bound**: The classical birthday problem calculates the number of people required before two share a birthday. The answer, twenty-three people for even odds across 365 days, often surprises newcomers because the number of potential pairs grows quadratically with group size. Systems designers exploit this same relation to size hash tables and cryptographic identifiers. In multi-agent execution, each worker represents an individual and the repository files represent the calendar, with collision probability amplified because each worker samples $\\WorkerWrittenFileCount$ distinct files simultaneously rather than a single day.\n\nProviding each worker with an isolated workspace establishes a stable snapshot baseline and an unshared scratchpad for local modifications. When a worker finishes, the runtime therefore receives an immutable candidate artifact (identified by a cryptographic commit hash) rather than a reference to a mutable filesystem directory. Runtimes implement this isolation through two main mechanisms. Linked Git worktrees allocate a dedicated working tree, index, and `HEAD` pointer for each agent while sharing underlying object storage in `.git/objects`. Copy-on-write filesystems provide block-level cloning and zero-copy snapshots governed by runtime pooling lifecycles and reset contracts.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ Copy-on-write provisioning, which copies only what a task writes over a read-only base, is established in @sec-vol3-sandboxes-cow-provisioning.\n:::\n\nNeither filesystem mechanism provides security on its own. If an agent executes arbitrary shell commands with unrestricted filesystem permissions, it can traverse parent directories, access peer worktrees, or corrupt the shared Git directory. A robust architecture therefore pairs workspace isolation with an external sandbox and a mediated tool gateway. The gateway confines filesystem writes to the assigned worktree, strips integration credentials, and allocates ephemeral ports and temporary directories for test suites. Discarding a worktree also cleanly recycles local ephemeral state, but it cannot revoke side effects that an external service has already accepted.\n\n### Dependency validation at integration {#sec-vol3-multiagent-dependency-validation}\n\nWhen a worker signals completion, the centralized integration service evaluates the returned execution receipt. The service resolves `runtime.base_ref` and `runtime.candidate_ref`, verifies cryptographic commit lineage, and independently derives the modified file paths rather than trusting a worker-reported write set. Beyond commit hashes, the service inspects `runtime.dependencies_ref`, which records the exact source versions, environmental flags, and file reads underpinning the candidate. Reliable dependency tracking requires runtime instrumentation of mediated filesystem calls, lockfiles, and configuration manifests; subjective, model-generated self-reports cannot be trusted for correctness. Furthermore, negative dependencies require careful tracking: an agent&#x27;s code generation may depend on the *absence* of a conflicting file, meaning that recording only positive file reads yields incomplete dependency graphs. Whenever dependency tracking is partial or ambiguous, the integration service treats an empty dependency manifest as unknown rather than disjoint, triggering a fresh revalidation of the candidate against the current branch head.\n\nLet the commit content hashes $\\WorkerBaseCommitHash$, $\\WorkerCandidateCommitHash$, and $\\IntegrationHeadCommitHash$ identify the worker&#x27;s base, its returned candidate, and the integration head captured for this attempt. The protocol has four steps, which @lst-vol3-occ-commit condenses into the control flow of an integration service.\n\n1. **Revalidate dependencies**: Compare dependency versions between $\\WorkerBaseCommitHash$ and $\\IntegrationHeadCommitHash$. Pairwise disjoint file sets demonstrate only textual independence. If a referenced interface or schema changed on the main branch, the runtime must revise or regenerate the candidate before attempting integration.\n2. **Build the candidate**: Construct an immutable integration candidate $\\IntegrationCandidateCommitHash$. If $\\IntegrationHeadCommitHash$ still matches $\\WorkerBaseCommitHash$, the candidate is the worker&#x27;s own commit $\\WorkerCandidateCommitHash$ (the fast-forward path). If the head advanced, the service stages a private merge commit combining $\\IntegrationHeadCommitHash$ and $\\WorkerCandidateCommitHash$. Should textual or structural conflicts arise, the service may dispatch a bounded reconciliation task to an agent. The resulting merge artifact is treated as a fresh candidate proposal, subject to the full validation pipeline.\n3. **Check the exact candidate**: Every integration candidate must pass the scope and quality checks mandated by `migration_accept_v1` within the protected assessment sandbox of @sec-vol3-evaluation-gyms-grader-isolation. Under the evidence-scope rule, verification tests executed on the worker&#x27;s private branch do not validate the newly synthesized merge candidate. Acceptance criteria must be evaluated directly on the exact commit hash $\\IntegrationCandidateCommitHash$.\n4. **Publish with an expected-head update**: Advance the target ref if and only if the current branch pointer still equals the captured $\\IntegrationHeadCommitHash$ and all publication guards remain satisfied. These guards verify active grant authorization, unrevoked task status, comprehensive dependency tracking, and fresh acceptance test evidence. In Git, the low-level `update-ref` command with an expected old object hash guarantees an atomic compare-and-swap. The runtime sequences authorization guards with this atomic ref update to eliminate race conditions between token revocation and publication, while external dependencies are anchored by their action-time version contracts.\n\nIf a concurrent writer advances the branch head before the atomic ref update executes, the integration attempt aborts. Rather than blindly retrying the update against the new head, the service loops back to step 1, revalidating all dependencies against the updated state. If the publication command returns an ambiguous network outcome or timeout, the service must query the authoritative repository log before attempting a retry, preventing duplicate commits. @Fig-vol3-occ-worktrees assembles three ingredients: private worktrees over shared repository storage, a staged merge that is textually clean even though the caller relies on the interface as of $\\WorkerBaseCommitHash$, and the four-step gate whose moved-head exit returns to revalidation. @Tbl-vol3-occ-dependency-trace then follows the caller worker&#x27;s patch across each decision boundary.\n\n::: {#lst-vol3-occ-commit lst-cap=&quot;**Guarded Integration of a Worker Candidate**: The service revalidates dependencies against the captured head, checks the exact candidate, and publishes with an expected-head update under the task&#x27;s publication guard.&quot;}\n</code></pre>\n<p>def integrate(repo, task, receipt, contract, max_attempts): for attempt in range(max_attempts): head = repo.read_ref(TARGET)                          # h_head for this attempt stale = changed_dependencies(receipt.dependencies_ref, receipt.base_ref, head) if stale: return request_revision(task, stale, head)        # regenerate, then return if head == receipt.base_ref: candidate = receipt.candidate_ref                 # fast-forward case else: candidate = stage_merge(head, receipt.candidate_ref) if candidate is None: return request_reconciliation(task, head)     # another proposal evidence = run_protected_checks(contract, candidate)  # binds this exact candidate if not evidence.accepted: return reject(task, candidate, evidence) with publication_guard(task) as guard:                # cancellation, grants, lineage if not guard.eligible: return withdrawn(task) outcome = repo.update_ref(TARGET, candidate, expected=head) if outcome.uncertain: return reconcile_publication(task, candidate)     # never blind-retry the effect if outcome.applied: return published(task, candidate, evidence)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">head moved: loop back to dependency validation for the new head</h1>\n<p>return unresolved(task, reason=\"integration_attempts_exhausted\")</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>\n:::\n\n::: {#fig-vol3-occ-worktrees data-explore=&quot;original-v3-occ-worktrees&quot; data-explore-key=&quot;vol3:fig-vol3-occ-worktrees&quot; fig-env=&quot;figure&quot; fig-pos=&quot;htb&quot; fig-cap=&quot;**Private Workspaces and Guarded Publication**: Linked worktrees (a) give each worker its own files, index, and HEAD while sharing repository storage; the sandbox around each one, not the worktree, scopes its writes. The caller edits files the interface change never touched (b), so the staged merge is textually clean even though the caller relies on the interface as of $\\WorkerBaseCommitHash$. Publication (c) therefore revalidates dependencies at the captured head, checks the exact integration candidate, and advances the target only if the head is unchanged and the publication guard holds. A moved head returns the attempt to validation, and every other exit returns an explicit status.&quot; fig-alt=&quot;Three panels. Panel a: a shared repository storage box linked to an interface worktree and a caller worktree, each holding files, index, and HEAD inside a dashed amber sandbox. Panel b: a commit graph in which h base has an interface-change arrow to h head and a caller-edits arrow to h worker; a dashed red arrow labeled stale dependency points from h worker back to h base; h head and h worker both lead to h candidate, the worker by a staged merge. Panel c: four numbered steps in a row, 1 revalidate h base vs. h head, 2 build candidate makes h candidate, 3 checks on h candidate, and 4 atomic update expects h head and the guard, ending in published with evidence; each step has a red exit, request revision, request reconciliation, reject, and reconcile outcome, and a dashed arrow labeled head moved returns from step 4 to step 1.&quot;}\n\n:::\n\n| Integration boundary                            | Evidence available                                                     | Required decision                                             |\n|:------------------------------------------------|:-----------------------------------------------------------------------|:--------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Both workers start at `repo_base_01`            | Caller depends on the original interface                               | Keep each candidate private                                   |\n| Interface change becomes `repo_head_02`         | Caller writes are separate, but its interface dependency changed       | Revalidate or regenerate affected caller work                 |\n| Revised caller candidate passes required checks | Results identify the exact combined candidate and checked inputs       | Attempt guarded publication against `repo_head_02`            |\n| Head changes before publication                 | Candidate evidence was established for the earlier integration attempt | Return to validation; do not overwrite the intervening change |\n\n: **A Clean Merge with a Stale Dependency**: Publication requires current dependency support and exact-candidate evidence, even when the workers edit different files. {#tbl-vol3-occ-dependency-trace}\n\nRepeated invalidations quickly erode the efficiency gains of parallel delegation. If several agents continuously update overlapping dependencies, workers spend more compute discarding stale rollouts and reconciling candidates than generating productive code. Runtimes mitigate this through bounded retries, exponential backoff, and serialized commit queues, transitioning exhausted attempts into explicitly unresolved task states. However, when invalidation rates remain persistently high, the architectural remedy lies in work decomposition rather than concurrency tuning. Structuring the task graph to sequence interface migrations ahead of dependent caller tasks eliminates the cross-worker hazard entirely, achieving higher net throughput with fewer wasted agent turns.\n\n### Leases for external resources {#sec-vol3-multiagent-external-leases}\n\nRepository staging isolates and buffers only local repository state. A failed Git integration rolls back local commits, but it cannot undo mutations already executed on shared relational databases, physical test rigs, or external payment gateways. External resources operate under their own conditional-update and ownership semantics.\n\nWhen managing unmergeable stateful resources (a live database migration lock, a dedicated test bench, or an embedded hardware debugger), the runtime must decide precisely when to enforce mutual exclusion. A **pessimistic lease** acquires an exclusive, time-bounded grant before an agent begins work, and the coordinator reclaims an expired grant so that a stalled holder cannot keep the resource indefinitely. Conflicting workers block until the lease holder releases the lock or times out, avoiding conflict-driven discarded work while the lease remains valid, but agent concurrency collapses into serialized execution. Conversely, *optimistic control* maximizes parallel execution by deferring conflict checks until publication. If validation fails, however, the runtime discards the entire execution trajectory, while any external side effects already dispatched to outside systems remain irreversible. This irreversibility governs the architectural division: optimistic concurrency control excels for private, discardable working trees, whereas the non-mergeable exclusive resources considered here require exclusion established at the target resource before issuing commands. @Tbl-vol3-concurrency-controls compares these operational strategies.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ Leases, the split-brain they permit when a stalled worker outlives its grant, and epoch fencing at the destination resource are established in @sec-vol3-tool-calling-operation-ids.\n:::\n\n| Control                                   | Exclusion established                            | Cost of a conflict                       | Parallelism                              | Fits                                         | Residual hazard                                                       |\n|:------------------------------------------|:-------------------------------------------------|:-----------------------------------------|:-----------------------------------------|:---------------------------------------------|:----------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Pessimistic lease with fencing            | Before acting, at the resource                   | Waiting; avoids conflict-driven discard  | Serialized per resource                  | Non-mergeable resources and external effects | Stalled holders; expiry needs a resource-side fence                   |\n| Optimistic validation at publication      | At publication, against the captured head        | Discarded trajectory and repeated checks | Workers proceed concurrently             | Private, discardable candidates              | Repeated invalidation under contention                                |\n| Optimistic validation with reconciliation | At publication, followed by a bounded merge task | Reconciliation work and a new check      | Concurrent, plus the reconciliation work | Coupled changes that usually compose         | A reconciled candidate is another proposal that needs full validation |\n\n: **Where Exclusion Is Established**: Leases pay in waiting before the work; optimistic control pays in discarded or reconciled work afterward, which only private candidates can afford. {#tbl-vol3-concurrency-controls}\n\nChecking lease validity inside the runtime before dispatching a command is not enough on its own, because an agent stalled by rate limits or slow inference can outlive its lease while a previously dispatched write is still in transit. Each leased resource therefore needs the resource-side fencing token of @sec-vol3-durable-execution-availability-ownership, which stamps every lease transfer with a monotonically increasing generation number and rejects any write carrying an older generation. While the generation number fences out stale holders after a lease changes hands, a duplicate of the same operation sent by the current holder remains a separate hazard that the runtime settles through its operation identity under settlement before retry (principle \\ref{pri-vol3-exactly-once-settlement}). Delegation links these mechanisms whenever a supervisor restarts a failed or stalled worker under the policies of @sec-vol3-multiagent-supervision-trees, because the old attempt and the replacement attempt are siblings targeting the same external resource. Before the supervisor releases the replacement worker to issue commands, the runtime must advance the lease generation at the resource itself so that any late write from the superseded sibling is rejected at the destination.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↪ A delayed writer, traced before and after its replacement&#x27;s fencing token is installed, is developed in @sec-vol3-appendix-systems-fencing.\n:::\n\nGuarded publication provides a robust concurrency framework for serializing concurrent state transitions. Its scope, however, is limited to structural and dependency consistency; OCC does not establish semantic correctness. If multiple workers inherit the same flawed assumptions from a shared planner, each worker can satisfy every step of the guarded publication protocol and cleanly commit a flawed patch to the repository.\n\n## Correlated Ensemble Failures {#sec-vol3-multiagent-consensus}\n\nAdding a panel of five model reviewers to `migration_task_01` to check semantic correctness before publication exposes the same shared-assumption hazard. All five reviewers may endorse `worker_caller_01`&#x27;s patch because they share the mistaken assumption that the legacy interface remains available, accumulating approvals without ever verifying the underlying dependency. This synchronized breakdown illustrates a **correlated failure**, in which the reviewers fail on the same inputs. When individual agents share systemic blind spots, consensus among them is informative only up to the limits of their joint error distribution.\n\n### Shared difficulty and conditional independence {#sec-vol3-multiagent-shared-difficulty}\n\nTwo classical foundations in fault tolerance appear to promise the opposite. Condorcet&#x27;s jury theorem [@condorcet1785essai] states that if independent voters are each correct with probability above one half, the probability that a majority vote is correct approaches one as voters are added. In computer engineering, N-modular redundancy applies the identical arithmetic to hardware, masking a faulty replica through majority voting circuits [@vonneumann1956probabilistic]. Both guarantees rest on a basic premise: that individual voters or components fail independently given the ground truth. Software engineering discovered during the 1980s that this premise breaks down in practice. Independently developed program versions failed on identical inputs far more frequently than statistical independence predicted [@knight1986experimental]. @Tbl-vol3-nmr-vs-review-ensembles contrasts the physical roots of independence in hardware redundancy with the fragile assumptions underlying model reviewer ensembles.\n\n| Question                | Replicated hardware under majority vote                      | Model reviewers under majority vote                                            |\n|:------------------------|:-------------------------------------------------------------|:-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Source of independence  | Physically separate faults such as particle strikes and wear | Must be established; shared training, prompts, and inputs can correlate errors |\n| What a replica checks   | The same deterministic computation                           | A judgment about a candidate whose difficulty is shared by every reviewer      |\n| What adding voters buys | Majority error falls rapidly when faults are independent     | Gain depends on the joint error model and may level off at a floor             |\n\n: **Redundancy with and without Independent Faults**: Majority voting inherits its guarantee from independent failures, an assumption that must be justified for both replicated hardware and model reviewers. {#tbl-vol3-nmr-vs-review-ensembles}\n\n: **N-Version Programming**: N-version programming builds several distinct implementations of a software specification and votes on their runtime outputs. Knight and Leveson had twenty-seven versions written independently across two universities and evaluated each against one million test inputs; although individual versions achieved high reliability, inputs on which several failed together were far more common than independence allowed [@knight1986experimental]. Language models trained on overlapping web-scale data face this exact vulnerability: benchmark evaluations across hundreds of models show heavily correlated errors, with the strongest correlation appearing among the largest and most capable models [@kim2025correlated].\n\nTo quantify how correlation degrades redundancy, consider a binary review process evaluating candidate artifacts known to violate an acceptance criterion. Let $\\ReviewerFalseApprovalIndicator_j=1$ denote the event where reviewer $\\ReviewerIndex$ erroneously approves an invalid candidate, with marginal false-approval probability $\\MarginalFalseApprovalProbability$ over this candidate population. If an ensemble of $\\WorkerCount$ reviewers makes independent errors with identical probability $\\MarginalFalseApprovalProbability$, their total number of false approvals $\\FalseApprovalCount$ follows a binomial distribution. For an odd ensemble size $\\WorkerCount$, a faulty majority forms with probability\n\n$$\n\\Pr(\\FalseApprovalCount&gt;\\WorkerCount/2)=\\sum_{k=(\\WorkerCount+1)/2}^{\\WorkerCount}\\binom{\\WorkerCount}{\\FalseApprovalOutcomeCount}\\MarginalFalseApprovalProbability^\\FalseApprovalOutcomeCount(1-\\MarginalFalseApprovalProbability)^{\\WorkerCount-\\FalseApprovalOutcomeCount}.\n$$\n\nStatistical independence must always be stated relative to the information held fixed. Repeated, independently sampled calls to a single model on one fixed input prompt can be conditionally independent. However, candidate defects vary widely in real workloads: some bugs are subtle and intricate, whereas others are straightforward. Because a challenging candidate is difficult for every reviewer, individual reviewer errors become positively correlated across the broader candidate population. Multi-agent debate protocols also introduce an intentional communication channel where agents revise their answers after inspecting peer conclusions [@du2023improving]. This deliberate coupling introduces a secondary layer of dependence on top of shared task difficulty.\n\nA latent-propensity model makes this shared difficulty explicit. Eckhardt and Lee originally introduced this framework to explain coincident software failures [@eckhardt1985theoretical], assigning each candidate input an intrinsic propensity $\\FalseApprovalPropensity \\in [0, 1]$ of causing a randomly chosen version to fail. In an agent review setting, draw a candidate with false-approval propensity $\\FalseApprovalPropensity$. Conditional on $\\FalseApprovalPropensity$, each reviewer independently issues an erroneous approval with probability $\\FalseApprovalPropensity$. Reviewers share the candidate&#x27;s intrinsic difficulty, even though their individual sampling realizations remain independent. On a candidate of propensity $\\FalseApprovalPropensity$, two distinct reviewers therefore err together with probability $\\FalseApprovalPropensity^2$. Averaging across the candidate population gives a marginal false-approval rate of $\\MarginalFalseApprovalProbability=\\mathbb{E}[\\FalseApprovalPropensity]$ and a joint error probability of $\\mathbb{E}[\\FalseApprovalPropensity^2]$, so subtracting $\\MarginalFalseApprovalProbability^2$ leaves a pairwise covariance equal to $\\operatorname{Var}(\\FalseApprovalPropensity)$, which divides by the marginal Bernoulli variance $\\MarginalFalseApprovalProbability(1-\\MarginalFalseApprovalProbability)$ to give the correlation coefficient $\\CorrelationCoefficient$:\n\n$$\n\\MarginalFalseApprovalProbability=\\mathbb{E}[\\FalseApprovalPropensity],\\qquad\n\\operatorname{Cov}(\\ReviewerFalseApprovalIndicator_i,\\ReviewerFalseApprovalIndicator_j)=\\operatorname{Var}(\\FalseApprovalPropensity),\\qquad\n\\CorrelationCoefficient=\\frac{\\operatorname{Var}(\\FalseApprovalPropensity)}{\\MarginalFalseApprovalProbability(1-\\MarginalFalseApprovalProbability)}\\quad(\\PeerReviewerIndex\\ne \\ReviewerIndex).\n$$\n\nTo model this variation analytically, assume $\\FalseApprovalPropensity\\sim\\operatorname{Beta}(\\PropensityPriorApprovalShape,\\PropensityPriorRejectionShape)$, a flexible density on $[0,1]$ with positive shape parameters $\\PropensityPriorApprovalShape$ and $\\PropensityPriorRejectionShape$. Its mean is $\\PropensityPriorApprovalShape/(\\PropensityPriorApprovalShape+\\PropensityPriorRejectionShape)$, and for reviewers who share $\\FalseApprovalPropensity$ the pairwise correlation works out to $1/(\\PropensityPriorApprovalShape+\\PropensityPriorRejectionShape+1)$, so a small $\\PropensityPriorApprovalShape+\\PropensityPriorRejectionShape$ means candidates differ widely in difficulty and errors correlate strongly. Setting the mean to the marginal rate and the correlation to the target gives $\\PropensityPriorApprovalShape+\\PropensityPriorRejectionShape=\\CorrelationCoefficient^{-1}-1$, which implies $\\PropensityPriorApprovalShape=\\MarginalFalseApprovalProbability(\\CorrelationCoefficient^{-1}-1)$ and $\\PropensityPriorRejectionShape=(1-\\MarginalFalseApprovalProbability)(\\CorrelationCoefficient^{-1}-1)$ for $0&lt;\\MarginalFalseApprovalProbability&lt;1$ and $0&lt;\\CorrelationCoefficient&lt;1$. Compounding the conditional binomial distribution over this beta prior yields the beta-binomial probability for $\\FalseApprovalOutcomeCount$ false approvals:\n\n$$\n\\Pr(\\FalseApprovalCount=\\FalseApprovalOutcomeCount)=\\binom{\\WorkerCount}{\\FalseApprovalOutcomeCount}\\frac{\\mathrm{B}(\\FalseApprovalOutcomeCount+\\PropensityPriorApprovalShape,\\WorkerCount-\\FalseApprovalOutcomeCount+\\PropensityPriorRejectionShape)}{\\mathrm{B}(\\PropensityPriorApprovalShape,\\PropensityPriorRejectionShape)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathrm{B}$ denotes the beta function. A stipulated marginal error rate and pairwise correlation do not uniquely determine the full joint distribution, and the beta family represents an idealized model for shared difficulty. Any production deployment must therefore empirically measure its joint error distribution directly on domain-specific candidates.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↪ The derivation of the beta-binomial false-approval count from the latent-propensity model is developed in @sec-vol3-appendix-math-correlated-quorum.\n:::\n\n::: {#nbk-17-the-illusion-of-independence-in .callout-notebook title=&quot;What the joint error model changes&quot;}\n\n**Problem**: Five reviewers assess known-invalid candidates, each with marginal false-approval probability $\\MarginalFalseApprovalProbability=0.20$. How often does a majority falsely approve if their errors are independent, and how often under the beta mixture at $\\CorrelationCoefficient=0.40$?\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Purpose: Compare two assumed joint error models at the same marginal rate.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Inputs: Five reviewers, marginal false approval 0.20, mixture correlation 0.40.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show: Majority and unanimous false-approval probabilities.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How: Binomial masses and a beta-binomial mixture evaluated with log gamma</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│      (Scenarios.FiveCorrelatedReviewers).</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>import math from mlsysim import Scenarios from mlsysim.fmt import check, fmt_int, fmt_percent from mlsysim.physics import calc_beta_binomial_pmf</p>\n<p>class ReviewCorrelation:</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">1. LOAD: dimensionless illustrative error-model inputs.</h1>\n<p>panel = Scenarios.FiveCorrelatedReviewers reviewers, failure_p, rho = panel.reviewers, panel.false_approval, panel.correlation check(isinstance(reviewers, int) and reviewers &gt; 0 and reviewers % 2 == 1, \"positive odd reviewer count\") check(0 &lt; failure_p &lt; 1 and 0 &lt; rho &lt; 1, \"interior mixture parameters\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">2. EXECUTE: integrate the conditional binomial with log beta functions.</h1>\n<p>independent = [] for k in range(reviewers + 1): independent.append(math.comb(reviewers, k) <em> failure_p</em><em>k </em> (1 - failure_p) ** (reviewers - k)) mixture = calc_beta_binomial_pmf(reviewers, failure_p, rho) independent_majority = panel.independent_majority(reviewers) mixture_majority = panel.correlated_majority(reviewers)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">3. GUARD: distributions normalize and preserve the stipulated marginal.</h1>\n<p>check(math.isclose(sum(independent), 1), \"binomial mass\") check(math.isclose(sum(mixture), 1), \"beta-binomial mass\") mixture_mean = 0.0 for k, mass in enumerate(mixture): mixture_mean += k * mass check(math.isclose(mixture_mean / reviewers, failure_p), \"marginal error\") check(independent_majority &lt; mixture_majority &lt; failure_p, \"scenario ordering\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">4. OUTPUT: percentages used below, with precision matching the comparison.</h1>\n<p>failure_p_str = fmt_percent(failure_p, precision=0, style=\"prose\") independent_majority_str = fmt_percent(independent_majority, precision=2, style=\"prose\") mixture_majority_str = fmt_percent(mixture_majority, precision=2, style=\"prose\") independent_unanimous_str = fmt_percent(independent[-1], precision=3, style=\"prose\") mixture_unanimous_str = fmt_percent(mixture[-1], precision=2, style=\"prose\") unanimous_ratio_str = fmt_int(round(mixture[-1] / independent[-1])) ]```</p>\n<p><strong>Math</strong>: Summing the binomial masses for three or more false approvals gives <code>[ReviewCorrelation.independent_majority_str]</code> under independent errors. The beta-binomial masses with $\\PropensityPriorApprovalShape=\\MarginalFalseApprovalProbability(\\CorrelationCoefficient^{-1}-1)$ and $\\PropensityPriorRejectionShape=(1-\\MarginalFalseApprovalProbability)(\\CorrelationCoefficient^{-1}-1)$ give <code>[ReviewCorrelation.mixture_majority_str]</code>. Unanimous false approval changes from <code>[ReviewCorrelation.independent_unanimous_str]</code> to <code>[ReviewCorrelation.mixture_unanimous_str]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Systems insight</strong>: At the same marginal error rate, positive correlation erases most of the reliability gains expected from voting. The mixture's majority improves only slightly on a single reviewer's marginal rate $\\MarginalFalseApprovalProbability$, and a unanimous false approval is about <code>[ReviewCorrelation.unanimous_ratio_str]</code> times as likely as independence predicts. Before adding reviewers to an ensemble, estimate $\\CorrelationCoefficient$ empirically on known-invalid candidates. When correlation is high, spending additional token budget on more reviewers of the same type yields diminishing returns. That budget is better spent on independent, deterministic validation checks. These calculations condition on the candidate being invalid; the overall probability that an approved candidate is invalid also depends on the prevalence of invalid candidates in the workload.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ MAJORITY FALSE APPROVAL AS THE ENSEMBLE GROWS (LEGO)\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: Prose after notebook \\ref{nbk-17-the-illusion-of-independence-in} in\n# │          @sec-vol3-multiagent-consensus.\n# │ Goal: Show the floor that the assumed beta mixture places under majority\n# │       false approval, compared with independent reviewers.\n# │ Show: Majority false approval at a larger odd ensemble under both models,\n# │       and the limiting floor P(Q &gt; 1/2).\n# │ How: Reuse the mixture of ReviewCorrelation (Scenarios.FiveCorrelatedReviewers);\n# │      beta-binomial masses via log gamma; the floor is the regularized\n# │      incomplete beta mass of the propensity above 1/2.\n# │ Note: Reads ReviewCorrelation (calc-vol3-review-correlation) inputs only.\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import check, fmt_int, fmt_percent\n\nclass ReviewFloor:\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Same stipulated error model as the notebook) ────────────\n    panel = ReviewCorrelation.panel\n    failure_p = ReviewCorrelation.failure_p\n    rho = ReviewCorrelation.rho\n    large_ensemble = panel.large_ensemble\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (Majority tails and the limiting floor) ───────────────\n    independent_large = panel.independent_majority(large_ensemble)\n    mixture_large = panel.correlated_majority(large_ensemble)\n    floor = panel.floor\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Prose claims) ─────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(abs(ReviewCorrelation.mixture_majority - 0.1668) &lt; 0.001, &quot;Notebook majority value changed&quot;)\n    check(0.1490 &lt; floor &lt; 0.1500, &quot;Floor P(Q &gt; 1/2) drifted&quot;)\n    check(floor &lt; mixture_large &lt; ReviewCorrelation.mixture_majority, &quot;Mixture tail must decline toward the floor&quot;)\n    check(independent_large &lt; 0.01, &quot;Independent majority error must be small at this size&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────\n    large_ensemble_str = fmt_int(large_ensemble)\n    independent_large_str = fmt_percent(independent_large, precision=2, style=&quot;prose&quot;)\n    mixture_large_str = fmt_percent(mixture_large, precision=1, style=&quot;prose&quot;)\n    floor_str = fmt_percent(floor, precision=1, style=&quot;prose&quot;)\n]```\n\nThe mixture model also bounds what ensemble scaling can achieve. Conditional on a candidate&#x27;s propensity $\\FalseApprovalPropensity$, the weak law of large numbers implies that the fraction of reviewers falsely approving converges to $\\FalseApprovalPropensity$ as the ensemble grows. An arbitrarily large majority vote therefore falsely approves precisely those candidates with $\\FalseApprovalPropensity&gt;1/2$. Rather than decaying toward zero, majority false approval approaches a nonzero asymptotic floor at $\\Pr(\\FalseApprovalPropensity&gt;1/2)$.\n\nUnder the parameters modeled in the notebook, that floor is `[ReviewFloor.floor_str]`. At `[ReviewFloor.large_ensemble_str]` reviewers, independent errors would reduce majority false approvals to `[ReviewFloor.independent_large_str]`, whereas the beta mixture leaves the error rate stalled at `[ReviewFloor.mixture_large_str]`. In @fig-vol3-correlated-ensemble-failure the independent curve keeps falling toward zero while the mixture curve flattens just above the floor. At five reviewers the mixture moves probability to the extremes, so a candidate is approved by no one or by a majority far more often than independence predicts. Tripling the committee from five to `[ReviewFloor.large_ensemble_str]` reviewers of the same kind triples review spend yet lowers majority false approval only from `[ReviewCorrelation.mixture_majority_str]` to `[ReviewFloor.mixture_large_str]`, because the remaining errors concentrate on candidates that every such model finds difficult.\n\n::: {#fig-vol3-correlated-ensemble-failure fig-env=&quot;figure&quot; fig-pos=&quot;htb&quot; fig-cap=&quot;**Majority Review Under an Assumed Error Mixture**: Every reviewer has the same false-approval rate, $\\MarginalFalseApprovalProbability=0.20$. With independent errors (a), majority false approval at five reviewers (open markers) is `[ReviewCorrelation.independent_majority_str]` and keeps falling as reviewers are added. Under the beta mixture with $\\CorrelationCoefficient=0.40$ it is `[ReviewCorrelation.mixture_majority_str]` and levels off toward the floor $\\Pr(\\FalseApprovalPropensity&gt;1/2)$ of `[ReviewFloor.floor_str]`. At five reviewers (b), the mixture shifts probability toward the extremes, so a candidate is either approved by no one or approved by a majority far more often than independence predicts. The curves follow from an assumed beta mixture, not a measured law of model ensembles.&quot; fig-alt=&quot;Two panels. Panel a: line chart of majority false approval, 0 to 25 percent, against odd reviewer counts 1 to 15. The solid gray independent-errors line falls from 20 percent toward zero; the dashed red beta-mixture line, rho 0.40, falls only slightly and flattens just above a dotted red floor line near 15 percent labeled Pr of Q greater than one half. The five-reviewer points are open markers. Panel b: grouped bars for 0 to 5 reviewers approving an invalid candidate among five, solid gray for independent errors and red hatched for the beta mixture, with the region for 3 or more shaded as majority approves. The mixture&#x27;s bar at 0 is the tallest, near 57 percent, and its bars at 3, 4, and 5 exceed the independent ones.&quot;}\n\n:::\n\n### Evidence at the integration boundary {#sec-vol3-multiagent-integration-evidence}\n\nIn an automated workflow such as `migration_task_01`, five reviewers that all repeat the assumption that the old interface remains available provide essentially a single effective observation. Their consensus should not trigger an automatic merge; instead, it should trigger an end-to-end integration check that evaluates the modified caller alongside the updated callee interface. A protected, deterministic check supplies ground-truth evidence that model reviewers cannot fabricate. A compiler establishes whether the candidate compiles cleanly under the target configuration, and a regression test suite confirms that specified functional behaviors remain intact. If an untrusted worker agent modifies a test suite to bypass a signature verification check, the test may pass while completely failing to validate the security property. This vulnerability is why authoritative evaluation suites must remain isolated behind a protected grading boundary. Even so, deterministic validation remains bounded by test suite coverage gaps and flawed formal specifications.\n\nWithout such deterministic checks, classical distributed quorum protocols cannot rescue a review ensemble plagued by correlated errors. When a large language model (LLM) outputs well-formed, plausible, yet incorrect evidence, it produces a fail-plausible fault rather than a fail-stop crash. In the unsigned-message protocol model, Byzantine agreement guarantees consensus despite arbitrary faults only while faulty participants remain strictly below one third of the ensemble [@lamport1982byzantine]. Because reviewers that share training distributions, system prompts, or upstream reasoning summaries fail together, correlated errors can push the faulty fraction past that one-third threshold regardless of replica count. Requiring each reviewer to inspect primary evidence before viewing peer conclusions (information isolation) removes direct peer influence. Distinct model architectures, diversified prompts, and separate evidence sources lower $\\CorrelationCoefficient$ only as far as empirical measurement on known-invalid candidates demonstrates.\n\n: **Byzantine Bound**: The replica bound fixes how large a faulty fraction a protocol tolerates, not why replicas fail; the one-third limit applies to unsigned messages, and digital signatures relax it [@lamport1982byzantine]. Classical deployments seek to keep simultaneous faults below that bound through physical diversity, running distinct code bases on separate hardware in different administrative domains. Model reviewers drawn from common model families, or trained on overlapping web-scale data, lack that isolation by default.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ The fail-plausible fault, output that is well formed and plausible yet wrong, is established in @sec-vol3-intro-fail-plausible.\n:::\n\nConsensus among worker agents must never be confused with authoritative execution privilege. Reviewer agreement provides an informative heuristic signal within the bounds defined by an orchestration workflow. Merging code, publishing artifacts, broadening task scopes, or bypassing gate checks requires explicit capability grants issued by a privileged control plane. Even unanimous approval from the five reviewers in `migration_task_01` only advances the candidate to the integration gate, where the runtime still checks its scope, dependencies, and publication grant before updating the branch.\n\n::: {#chk-17-orphaned-worker-hazard .callout-checkpoint title=&quot;Interpreting concurrent results and agreement&quot;}\n\n- [ ] **Clean merge vs. valid change**: Why do disjoint write paths leave the caller patch unvalidated after an interface change, and which record lets the integration service decide?\n- [ ] **Collision estimate**: A repository has 2,000 files and each worker writes 10. Using @eq-vol3-multiagent-write-collision, roughly how many workers make a shared write more likely than not? Should the runtime isolate workers at that size?\n- [ ] **Reviewer budget**: Majority false approval is `[ReviewCorrelation.mixture_majority_str]` at five reviewers and levels off near `[ReviewFloor.floor_str]`. Should the system provision ten more reviewers of the same kind, or one protected test, and why?\n- [ ] **Approval vs. authority**: Distinguish a worker&#x27;s approval, a scoped protected-test result, and permission to publish the candidate.\n:::\n\nValidation evidence also carries a finite lifetime while asynchronous workers execute in parallel. If an upstream orchestrator rejects an intermediate candidate patch or alters its task plan, downstream worker agents may continue computing on stale state. Robust agent runtimes must invalidate and reject superseded work while properly terminating and accounting for in-flight child processes.\n\n## Bounding In-Flight Work {#sec-vol3-multiagent-backpressure}\n\nSuppose the supervisor cancels `migration_task_01` while `worker_interface_01` awaits an external test job and `worker_caller_01` finishes generating a candidate patch. Even if the caller&#x27;s patch passes its local validation checks after the supervisor initiates cancellation, the runtime must ensure that the patch remains strictly unpublishable. Meanwhile, an external testing service may have already accepted the interface worker&#x27;s test request, incurring resource and billing charges that remain unsettled long after the worker process halts. In single-agent execution, a cancellation signal interrupts a local loop or an outstanding remote inference call. In a multi-agent system, that control boundary must span an entire hierarchy of distributed descendants.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ Pause and cancellation of a single trajectory, applied at turn boundaries and by streaming cancellation, are established in @sec-vol3-controlplane-signals.\n:::\n\nTo enforce cancellation across a fleet of workers, the runtime records the affected subtree, its current control generation, and the rationale for stopping. Authoritative dispatch and publication gates consult this record along the serialized path that admits new work. In distributed agent systems, closing admission, terminating execution, and releasing resources are three distinct milestones, each demanding its own independent evidence. Everything admitted before the cancellation stays under the settlement rule of failure recovery, now applied across the entire subtree. An operation admitted before the stop preserves its identity, execution status, and assigned owner until its outcome and associated financial charges settle. No cancellation signal, authorization revocation, timeout, or queue purge can retroactively erase that obligation.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ The settlement rule, which keeps an operation&#x27;s reservation and record until evidence establishes its outcome, is established in @sec-vol3-sagas-acid-boundary.\n:::\n\n### Cancellation context trees {#sec-vol3-multiagent-cancellation-trees}\n\nWhen a parent task is canceled, a grandchild worker that has not yet received the stop signal can keep calling tools and publishing results under the parent&#x27;s delegated authority. The cancellation context tree prevents that leak without introducing a fourth graph structure. It is the supervision tree of @sec-vol3-multiagent-supervision-trees seen from the control plane, with the same parent-child ownership edges, each annotated with the control generation of the ancestor that governs the child. A child&#x27;s *delegation lineage* is its path from that tree&#x27;s root. Cancellation proceeds in two steps across the runtime&#x27;s graphs. First, the runtime traces delegation lineage rather than data-dependency edges to determine which active workers must lose their authority and budget. Because execution mandates and resource allowances flow down the supervision tree, revoking an ancestor immediately strips every descendant in that subtree of eligibility to run tools or publish results. Second, once that subtree is marked ineligible, the runtime inspects the data-dependency graph to see which tasks outside the canceled subtree were waiting on its outputs. Any downstream continuation that required an artifact from the canceled subtree can no longer satisfy its preconditions and is invalidated, whereas sibling or downstream tasks that treated the canceled child as an optional speculative branch continue undisturbed.\n\n::: {#dfn-cancellation-context-tree .callout-definition title=&quot;Hierarchical cancellation context tree&quot;}\n\n***Hierarchical cancellation context tree*** is a runtime-owned delegation structure that links each child to the ancestor control decisions governing its continued execution and result publication.\n\n1. **Significance**: It identifies which descendants must lose eligibility when an ancestor stops and which outstanding operations still need an owner.\n2. **Distinction**: Propagating a stop request changes control policy; confirming remote termination, resource release, and effect settlement requires separate evidence.\n3. **Common pitfall**: Treating a canceled parent record as proof that every child, external job, and charge has ended.\n\n:::\n\nThe ordering of events at each admission and publication gate dictates the precise boundary of cancellation. Suppose an admission gateway accepts `worker_interface_01`&#x27;s test operation, logs its unique operation identity along with its resource reservation, and begins transmitting the request. If the parent task cancels before a reply arrives, that test operation remains admitted and must proceed through the settlement protocol. However, any subsequent request from either child that arrives at that gateway after the cancellation decision is serialized will be rejected immediately. Result publication follows the exact same linear order. The integration path must re-verify task eligibility at the atomic publication boundary: if the cancellation commits first, it blocks publication of any patch, regardless of whether that patch passed every local check. If publication commits before the cancellation is serialized at the boundary, the resulting state change stands as a durable modification that the recovery contract must handle.\n\nUnder this architecture, a supervisor can authoritatively report that its gateways deny new work and reject publications without falsely claiming that a disconnected or remote worker process has already halted. A remote enforcement gateway that loses connectivity to the central control plane must fail closed. If a gateway instead relies on cached authorization tokens, it operates under an explicit stale-authorization window and cannot guarantee immediate, subtree-wide revocation (@sec-vol3-multiagent-authority).\n\n### Evidence preservation during cleanup {#sec-vol3-multiagent-cleanup-evidence}\n\nClosing a local client stream does not confirm that a remote model provider has ceased generation, and killing a local worker process leaves requests accepted by external services in flight. Because local termination and remote completion are asymmetric across distributed boundaries, the runtime must dispatch explicit stop requests to each reachable execution service, with each acknowledgment certifying only its specific operational scope (@tbl-vol3-cancellation-layers). An executor&#x27;s process-level or infrastructure confirmation always outranks an agent&#x27;s self-reported claim that it has halted.\n\n| Evidence                                 | What it establishes                                                                   | What remains separate                                              |\n|:-----------------------------------------|:--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|:-------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Authoritative admission hold             | Later model/tool admissions and candidate publication are denied at the covered gates | Previously admitted work and disconnected enforcement points       |\n| Executor confirms worker termination     | The identified execution scope has stopped under that executor&#x27;s contract             | Remote jobs, external effects, and final usage                     |\n| Provider confirms an operation&#x27;s outcome | The identified operation has the reported effect status                               | Other attempts, cleanup operations, and unresolved charges         |\n| Resource owner confirms release          | The identified slot, sandbox, or allocation is available again                        | Retention of required evidence and settlement of other obligations |\n\n: **Evidence at cancellation boundaries**: Each acknowledgment supports a scoped claim rather than certifying that the whole subtree has disappeared. {#tbl-vol3-cancellation-layers}\n\nFor an outstanding test request, the runtime retains the operation identity, execution attempt history, resource reservation, and external job handle while actively reconciling status. If the external test service eventually delivers a late result, that response can settle the operation&#x27;s outcome and finalize its billing without reactivating the canceled task trajectory. If the external outcome remains indeterminate, the runtime transfers the operation to a designated settlement owner. The execution receipt preserves these audit records through `runtime.settlement_ref`, ensuring they survive any context-window summarization or lineage compaction.\n\nCleanup operations cannot execute under the revoked worker&#x27;s authority; the runtime must provision a separately scoped administrative credential and a protected resource allowance. Tearing down an allocated environment is also an asynchronous operation with an uncertain outcome. Scratch workspace or container deletion must block until all relevant filesystem writers have terminated and required diagnostic artifacts have been safely archived. An asynchronous background sweeper reconciles registered physical allocations against these pending evidentiary obligations before unlinking directories or recycling sandbox containers.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ The cleanup graph that orders this teardown is established in @sec-vol3-failure-recovery-cleanup-graph.\n:::\n\n### Hedged requests and speculative work {#sec-vol3-multiagent-hedged-requests}\n\n\n\nBeyond handling failures and user aborts, cancellation is a primitive for speculative execution and latency management. When an agent workflow fans out across multiple subtasks, waiting for every parallel worker exposes the entire system to stragglers, the tail-at-scale problem [@dean2013tail]. Consider $\\WorkerCount$ parallel workers with independent and identically distributed completion times $\\WorkerCompletionTime_i$ required to produce an admissible result. If $\\WorkerCompletionDistribution(\\TimeInstant)=\\Pr(\\WorkerCompletionTime_i\\le \\TimeInstant)$ represents the cumulative probability of a single worker finishing within time $\\TimeInstant$, an **all-results barrier**, which requires every worker to complete, achieves a deadline completion probability of\n\n$$\\Pr\\bigl(\\max_i \\WorkerCompletionTime_i\\le \\TimeInstant\\bigr)=\\WorkerCompletionDistribution(\\TimeInstant)^{\\WorkerCount}.$$\n\nBecause the barrier needs every worker&#x27;s admissible result, each added worker multiplies the deadline probability by another factor $\\WorkerCompletionDistribution(\\TimeInstant)&lt;1$, so it decays geometrically as the fan-out $\\WorkerCount$ grows. This is the same geometric decay that horizon compounding (@eq-vol3-horizon-compounding) applies across turns, now applied across workers. In production systems, correlated queueing delays, shared rate limits, and unequal prompt lengths break the idealized independent model, so deadline probabilities must be measured under the shared serving conditions. However, if the task contract permits any $\\RequiredResultCount$ interchangeable admissible results out of $\\WorkerCount$ replicas, the probability of meeting the deadline becomes\n\n$$\\Pr(\\WorkerCompletionTime_{(k)}\\le \\TimeInstant)=\\sum_{j=k}^{\\WorkerCount}\\binom{\\WorkerCount}{\\FinishedWorkerCount} \\WorkerCompletionDistribution(\\TimeInstant)^\\FinishedWorkerCount[1-\\WorkerCompletionDistribution(\\TimeInstant)]^{\\WorkerCount-\\FinishedWorkerCount},$$\n\nwhere $\\WorkerCompletionTime_{(k)}$ denotes the arrival time of the $\\RequiredResultCount$th admissible result. This $\\RequiredResultCount$-of-$\\WorkerCount$ stopping rule applies strictly to interchangeable outputs, such as parallel candidate retrieval or redundant reasoning paths. In the migration scenario, by contrast, the system requires both the interface update and the caller refactor; no majority of completed caller patches can stand in for a missing interface patch.\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ DEADLINE COMPLETION UNDER AN EXPLICIT INDEPENDENCE MODEL</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: @sec-vol3-multiagent-backpressure, notebook immediately below.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal: Compare two stopping rules when the contract permits k of N_workers results.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show: Scenario inputs and probabilities of sufficient results by a deadline.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How: Evaluate the binomial tail (Scenarios.EightWorkerDeadlineFanout) and</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│      independently enumerate all outcomes.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Note: Hypothetical probabilities, not measured agent or provider latency.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from itertools import product from mlsysim import Scenarios from mlsysim.core.units import second from mlsysim.fmt import check, fmt_int, fmt_percent, fmt_time</p>\n<p>class FanoutCompletion:</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Scenario assumptions) ───────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>fanout = Scenarios.EightWorkerDeadlineFanout workers = fanout.workers required = fanout.required deadline = fanout.deadline admissible_probability = fanout.on_time_probability</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>all_probability = fanout.all_on_time</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">Smallest fan-out whose all-results barrier falls below even odds.</h1>\n<p>even_odds_workers = fanout.even_odds_workers enough_probability = fanout.quorum_on_time enumeration_probability = 0.0 total_probability = 0.0 for outcomes in product((0, 1), repeat=workers): completed = sum(outcomes) weight = admissible_probability*<em>completed </em> (1 - admissible_probability) ** (workers - completed) total_probability += weight if completed &gt;= required: enumeration_probability += weight</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants and displayed results) ───────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(1 &lt;= required &lt;= workers, \"Completion count must fit the fleet\") check(abs(total_probability - 1.0) &lt; 1e-12, \"Outcome mass must sum to one\") check(abs(enough_probability - enumeration_probability) &lt; 1e-12, \"Binomial tail must match exhaustive enumeration\") check(abs(all_probability - 0.43046721) &lt; 1e-12, \"All-results probability drift\") check(abs(enough_probability - 0.96190821) &lt; 1e-12, \"Permitted stopping probability drift\") check(enough_probability &gt; all_probability, \"Earlier stopping must improve this deadline probability\") check(even_odds_workers == 7, \"Seven workers first drop the barrier below even odds\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ───────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>workers_str = fmt_int(workers) required_str = fmt_int(required) deadline_str = fmt_time(deadline, unit=second, precision=0) admissible_str = fmt_percent(admissible_probability, precision=0, style=\"prose\") all_str = fmt_percent(all_probability, precision=2, style=\"prose\") enough_str = fmt_percent(enough_probability, precision=2, style=\"prose\") even_odds_workers_str = fmt_int(even_odds_workers) ]```</p>\n<p><strong>Problem</strong>: A hypothetical read-only analysis task permits any <code>[FanoutCompletion.required_str]</code> interchangeable admissible results from <code>[FanoutCompletion.workers_str]</code> workers. Each worker independently has a <code>[FanoutCompletion.admissible_str]</code> probability of returning such a result within <code>[FanoutCompletion.deadline_str]</code> of dispatch, including its checks. What is the probability of having sufficient results by that deadline, compared with waiting for every worker?</p>\n<p><strong>Math</strong>: Requiring every result gives $\\WorkerCompletionDistribution(\\TimeInstant)^{\\WorkerCount}$, or <code>[FanoutCompletion.all_str]</code>. Summing the binomial probabilities for <code>[FanoutCompletion.required_str]</code> through <code>[FanoutCompletion.workers_str]</code> completions gives <code>[FanoutCompletion.enough_str]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Systems insight</strong>: An all-results barrier multiplies the deadline probability by $\\WorkerCompletionDistribution(\\TimeInstant)$ for every worker added; at this per-worker rate it drops below even odds at <code>[FanoutCompletion.even_odds_workers_str]</code> workers. Where the contract permits interchangeable results, a $\\RequiredResultCount$-of-$\\WorkerCount$ stopping rule recovers most of the lost probability. Where it does not, the fan-out itself must shrink or the per-worker tail must be cut. The workers still running after the rule fires consume resources until cancellation takes effect, and that work belongs in the cost account.</p>\n<p><strong>Hedging</strong> mitigates tail latency by dispatching a redundant replacement attempt after a specified delay while the original worker continues running, subsequently accepting whichever admissible result completes first [@dean2013tail]. Hedging functions smoothly for read-only analyses and sandboxed candidate patches where a single runtime publication gate arbitrates the winner and cancels redundant runners. However, speculative hedging introduces acute hazards if an agent executes external mutating actions. Unless the destination service implements a strict duplicate-effect or idempotency contract, a trailing or canceled worker can execute irreversible side effects before its cancellation signal is processed. A hedge of a mutating action is a retry issued before the first attempt has settled, which settlement before retry (principle \\ref{pri-vol3-exactly-once-settlement}) forbids unless both attempts carry one operation identity that the endpoint deduplicates. Speculative attempts also consume real token allowances and concurrency slots; dispatching hedges into a congested inference cluster amplifies queue depth and prolongs the very tail latencies the mechanism was intended to truncate.</p>\n<p>↩ Uncertain outcomes and the endpoint deduplication contracts that make a repeated mutation safe are established in @sec-vol3-tool-calling-idempotency.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Hedged Requests</strong>: Dean and Barroso's recipe defers the second request until the first has been outstanding longer than the 95th-percentile expected latency, which bounds the extra load to roughly the fraction of requests that are that slow. For agents the delay matters more than for storage reads, because each hedge is a full model trajectory with its own reservation, and a hedge issued too early doubles spend on the common case to shorten the rare one.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h3\">Credit-based flow control</h3>\n<p>Even when every child agent stays within its own token budget, a rate mismatch between stages of a pipeline can still destabilize the workflow. A fast producer that emits candidate changes faster than a downstream reviewer or test runner can drain them fills the queue between the two stages. As that backlog grows, candidates wait longer before inspection, so the repository state they were built against moves underneath them while they sit in line.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ PRODUCER/CONSUMER RATE MISMATCH (LEGO)\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: Prose in @sec-vol3-multiagent-credit-flow-control, before and\n# │          after the credit invariant.\n# │ Goal: Show why an unbounded queue diverges and how a credit window bounds\n# │       the time an item waits.\n# │ Show: Consumer service rate, queue growth over a horizon, the wait behind\n# │       that queue, and the Little&#x27;s-law residence under a fixed window.\n# │ How: mu = decode rate / tokens per item; dQ/dt = lambda - mu; residence\n# │      of a full window = W / mu.\n# │ Note: Stipulated rates for a hypothetical pipeline, not measured serving\n# │       (Scenarios.DiffReviewCreditWindow).\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import Scenarios, second, minute, hour\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import check, fmt_int, fmt_ratio, fmt_time\n\nclass RateMismatch:\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Stipulated rates) ───────────────────────────────────────\n    pipeline = Scenarios.DiffReviewCreditWindow\n    consumer_tokens_per_s = pipeline.decode_rate.to(&quot;token/s&quot;).magnitude\n    tokens_per_item = pipeline.tokens_per_item.to(&quot;token&quot;).magnitude\n    producer_items_per_s = pipeline.producer_rate.to(&quot;1/s&quot;).magnitude\n    horizon = pipeline.horizon\n    credits = pipeline.credits\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (Service rate, divergence, and bounded residence) ─────\n    service_rate = pipeline.service_rate.to(&quot;1/s&quot;).magnitude  # items/s\n    growth_rate = pipeline.growth_rate.to(&quot;1/s&quot;).magnitude  # items/s\n    backlog = pipeline.backlog  # items\n    backlog_wait = pipeline.backlog_wait\n    window_residence = pipeline.window_residence\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Prose claims) ─────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(abs(service_rate - 0.08) &lt; 1e-12, &quot;Consumer service rate&quot;)\n    check(growth_rate &gt; 0, &quot;Queue must diverge under this mismatch&quot;)\n    check(abs(backlog - 2952) &lt; 1e-6, &quot;Backlog after the horizon&quot;)\n    check(10 * hour &lt; backlog_wait &lt; 10.5 * hour, &quot;Wait behind the backlog is about ten hours&quot;)\n    check(abs(window_residence.to(second).magnitude - 100) &lt; 1e-9, &quot;Window residence&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ───────────────────────────────────────────\n    consumer_rate_str = fmt_int(consumer_tokens_per_s)\n    tokens_per_item_str = fmt_int(tokens_per_item)\n    producer_rate_str = fmt_int(producer_items_per_s)\n    service_rate_str = fmt_ratio(service_rate, precision=2)\n    horizon_str = fmt_time(horizon, unit=minute, precision=0, style=&quot;word&quot;)\n    backlog_str = fmt_int(backlog)\n    backlog_wait_str = fmt_time(backlog_wait, unit=hour, precision=1, style=&quot;word&quot;)\n    credits_str = fmt_int(credits)\n    window_residence_str = fmt_time(window_residence, unit=second, precision=0, style=&quot;word&quot;)\n]```\n\nConsider an upstream agent that lists files and emits candidate diffs at $\\ArrivalRate =$ `[RateMismatch.producer_rate_str]` items per second. A downstream reviewer model generates a `[RateMismatch.tokens_per_item_str]`-token critique for each item at a decode speed of `[RateMismatch.consumer_rate_str]` tokens per second, yielding an effective service rate of only $\\ServiceRate =$ `[RateMismatch.service_rate_str]` items per second. In an unconstrained queue, the backlog expands at the net rate $\\ArrivalRate - \\ServiceRate$. After `[RateMismatch.horizon_str]`, the queue accumulates `[RateMismatch.backlog_str]` unreviewed items, and a newly submitted diff faces an estimated queue wait of `[RateMismatch.backlog_wait_str]`. Long before process memory is exhausted, the queued items become obsolete, because the repository state they reference has diverged, reproducing between agents the stale-observation failure that context invalidation guards against inside one trajectory. Per-agent token budgets fail to prevent this collapse because each individual candidate item is inexpensive to generate.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ Context invalidation, which withdraws observations that a later change has made stale, is established in @sec-vol3-context-engineering-context-rot.\n:::\n\nA finite **credit window** prevents this divergence by bounding the number of admitted, in-flight items, much as an aggregate budget caps token expenditure. It is the same mechanism that bounds staged records in trajectory collection. A producer transmits only while it holds a credit the consumer issued for free capacity, so congestion delays the producer but never overflows a buffer. Let $\\CreditWindowCapacity$ denote the fixed credit capacity of the channel, $\\FreeCreditCount$ the unallocated credits held by the producer, and $\\OutstandingItemCount$ the outstanding admitted items currently circulating in the pipeline. The runtime enforces the structural invariant:\n\n$$\\FreeCreditCount+\\OutstandingItemCount=\\CreditWindowCapacity,\\qquad \\FreeCreditCount\\ge0,\\quad \\OutstandingItemCount\\ge0.$$\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ Collection credits, which bound staged trajectories between generation and verification, are established in @sec-vol3-trajectory-curation-collection-credits.\n:::\n\nUnder this discipline, admitting a new work item requires the producer to acquire and attach an available credit. That credit remains tied to the item while it traverses the network, waits in downstream queues, undergoes evaluation, or reserves output buffer space. The runtime returns the credit to the producer&#x27;s pool only when the item reaches a designated capacity-release boundary. Credit returns are processed atomically and with at-most-once semantics. A lost return silently shrinks the window, and a duplicated one inflates it, so network disconnections trigger a recount of outstanding credits against the authoritative runtime state rather than granting producers a fresh batch of credits.\n\nBeyond protecting buffer memory, credit windows provide a sizing model for mean residence at a sustained service rate. Little&#x27;s law (theorem \\ref{thrm-05-littles-law}, @eq-vol3-littles-law) states that the mean number of items held equals their admitted rate times their mean residence. Applying that relation to a saturated window maps the items held to the outstanding ones, $\\OutstandingItemCount=\\CreditWindowCapacity$. The window admits a new item only when the consumer drains one, so the admitted rate is the drain rate $\\ServiceRate$, not the producer&#x27;s offered rate $\\ArrivalRate$. Dividing the items held by the admitted rate gives a mean residence of $\\CreditWindowCapacity/\\ServiceRate$. With a window of $\\CreditWindowCapacity =$ `[RateMismatch.credits_str]` credits in that rate-mismatch pipeline, a diff that would have waited `[RateMismatch.backlog_wait_str]` behind the unbounded backlog instead resides in the window for `[RateMismatch.window_residence_str]` on average. A hard freshness requirement additionally needs admission timestamps, deadline checks before service, and bounded service times if timely service must be guaranteed. When credits are exhausted, the producer yields execution instead of flooding the pipeline with artifacts destined to be obsolete upon inspection. If each in-flight payload is constrained to at most $\\DataVolume$ bytes, total memory occupancy cannot exceed $\\CreditWindowCapacity\\DataVolume$. That memory bound demonstrates why payload return bounds (@sec-vol3-multiagent-contracts) serve as admission-control parameters rather than mere prompt formatting conventions.\n\nExhausting data credits, however, must never starve control-plane traffic. Signals such as cancellation commands, credit reconciliation messages, execution status receipts, and financial settlement records travel over reserved out-of-band control channels. The runtime must reject new task admissions whenever doing so would risk exhausting the execution slots reserved for downstream consumers and cleanup handlers. Otherwise, upstream producers waiting for credits will block the very consumers needed to release them, deadlocking the system. Finally, releasing a channel credit must not be conflated with releasing an external resource or refunding a financial commitment. Credit reclamation restores pipeline capacity, whereas financial settlement accounts for external cost.\n\nTogether, cancellation trees and credit windows bound the volume and financial exposure of in-flight work while keeping control paths responsive, and the credit window sets mean residence under the stated service rate. Neither control, however, decides which operations a child is allowed to propose while it runs. Enforcing those limits across a delegation tree depends on the authority model at each node: what capabilities each descendant holds, how its allowance is reserved, and how fresh its lineage must be when checked at a remote gateway (@sec-vol3-multiagent-authority).\n\n## Capability Delegation {#sec-vol3-multiagent-authority}\n\nSuppose `worker_caller_01`, an agent authorized only to update selected caller functions, spawns a new child task to inspect upstream source code and designates the root orchestrator as that child&#x27;s parent. If the runtime naively resolves authority from the client-supplied parent identifier, the inspection child inherits the root&#x27;s global authority, including publication credentials intended exclusively for the release integration service. The delegation tree (the supervision tree&#x27;s parent-child edges, along which grants now flow) has itself manufactured a confused deputy (definition \\ref{dfn-confused-deputy}). The runtime resolved authority from a name the requester supplied, the parent identifier that the child chose for itself. Similarly, two sibling workers dispatched concurrently might both observe the parent&#x27;s uncommitted budget before either issues an external call, overdrawing the task&#x27;s shared financial or token allowance. Task cancellation exposes a matching temporal hazard: a descendant grant that was perfectly valid when minted must admit no further work once the runtime revokes an ancestor in its lineage.\n\nScoped grants and credential brokering establish where authority resides within isolated execution environments. Multi-agent architectures extend this foundation through **attenuated delegation**: every hop down the task hierarchy passes authority no broader than the delegator&#x27;s existing authority. This model enforces the principle of least privilege [@saltzer1975] with capabilities (definition \\ref{dfn-capability}). The authority therefore travels in the reference a child holds rather than being looked up from the identity it claims, and confinement survives concurrent spending and ancestor state transitions. While a model may propose spawning a child task and suggest its scope, the runtime supervisor alone assigns the child&#x27;s identity, issues its attenuated grant, commits its resource reservation, and binds its lifecycle.\n\n### Attenuated grants and split allowances {#sec-vol3-multiagent-attenuated-grants}\n\nPassing a parent&#x27;s full credential or unreserved budget balance to a child lets a single compromised subtask modify unrelated files or spend the entire task allowance. To keep each hop narrower than the one above it, a delegation tree applies the containment relation $\\GrantAllowedAccesses(\\ChildCapabilityGrant)\\subseteq\\GrantAllowedAccesses(\\ParentCapabilityGrant)$ established in @sec-vol3-sandboxes-attenuation at every hop. In a multi-agent tree the relation governs concrete operations executed on underlying system resources, rather than superficial labels such as tool names or directory prefixes that an agent might attempt to manipulate. For example, an inspection child task may hold permission to inspect a specific interface file, but it must possess no rights to modify caller source files, exfiltrate repository data, or access the parent broker&#x27;s credentials for external repositories. The execution boundary enforces these perimeter restrictions directly; the child environment starts without inheriting broader host credentials or open file handles.\n\nRuntimes can implement these bindings using either protected internal kernel handles or cryptographically verifiable bearer tokens. Chained-caveat tokens let a holder attenuate its grant locally without contacting an issuer, because each added restriction extends a keyed hash chain that the holder cannot unwind [@birgisson2014macaroons]. Because the runtime supervisor, never the generative model, binds the resulting capability to the specific task execution context, a compromised or malfunctioning worker cannot forge a parent lineage or substitute a token belonging to another task.\n\nPermissions and consumable budgets require different delegation rules. Permissions are not depleted by use, so they can be shared: a parent that delegates read access retains its own ability to read. Consumable budgets, by contrast, are depletable resources that must be partitioned rather than duplicated, ensuring that each unit of currency or token quota is spent at most once. The admission control gate must therefore ensure that no unit of budget is created from nothing or spent twice anywhere in the delegation hierarchy. Before dispatching a child task, the runtime atomically reserves the child&#x27;s admitted liability against the shared allowance, accounting for all concurrent siblings and the parent&#x27;s own outstanding work. This atomic reservation prevents two sibling workers from racing to commit the same remaining balance. It is the shared-allowance rule of trajectory-level encapsulation (principle \\ref{pri-vol3-trajectory-encapsulation}) applied across a tree, under which concurrent holders reserve against one allowance and a derived allowance only narrows. If a child subsequently subdivides its allowance among its descendants, it creates a nested sub-budget that can never exceed its own allocation, not an independent pool that can be charged separately.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↪ The settlement rules developed in @sec-vol3-agent-economics-budget-conservation decide when a delegated allocation&#x27;s unspent backing returns to the parent and count a late bill once.\n:::\n\n### Revocation and lineage checks {#sec-vol3-multiagent-revocation-lineage}\n\nOnce attenuated grants and sub-budgets are issued down the tree, revoking them in a distributed multi-agent system cannot depend on promptly delivering push notifications to every running child. A robust architectural pattern assigns each task node in the runtime a monotonic generation counter and an operational state (such as active, canceled, or completed). When a grant is issued, the runtime binds it cryptographically to the complete root-to-holder lineage path, recording the exact generation counter of each ancestor at the moment of delegation. Revoking an ancestor marks that node ineligible or advances its generation counter. Node restarts must monotonically advance or preserve generation counters rather than resetting to zero, which would inadvertently revive expired or revoked grants.\n\nWhen an agent submits an operation to the API gateway, the admission check verifies the grant&#x27;s cryptographic integrity, scope, and expiration while querying the current lifecycle status and generation of every ancestor in the bound lineage path. If any ancestor has been canceled or its generation has advanced, the gateway rejects the descendant&#x27;s request immediately, even if the leaf worker has not yet received a cancellation signal and still considers itself active. Traversing a lineage path of depth $\\ell$ requires $\\mathcal{O}(\\ell)$ ancestor lookups at admission time, whereas advancing an ancestor&#x27;s generation number invalidates the entire subtree of descendant grants in $\\mathcal{O}(1)$ time without rewriting individual token payloads. Gateway caches or indexed summaries can shorten those admission lookups at the cost of cache-invalidation rules, and @tbl-vol3-gateway-epoch-table traces how the check behaves across `migration_task_01` without assuming that cancellation messages arrive everywhere at once.\n\n| Situation                                                   | What the admission authority knows                               | Consequence                                                                                                                         |\n|:------------------------------------------------------------|:-----------------------------------------------------------------|:------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| A worker proposes an operation before cancellation          | Bound lineage generations match and ancestor tasks remain active | Continue checking operation scope, resource conditions, expiry, and budget; lineage validity alone does not authorize the operation |\n| The root cancellation is committed before a later admission | The root is ineligible or its generation differs from the grant  | Reject the descendant request even if the worker has not received its cancellation message                                          |\n| A remote gateway cannot establish current ancestor state    | A cached record may predate cancellation                         | Withhold admission under the strict freshness policy; do not claim current authorization from the old record                        |\n| An external operation was admitted before cancellation      | Its outcome is still unresolved                                  | Retain operation identity and settlement ownership; rejecting later requests does not undo the earlier effect                       |\n\n: **Lineage checks across cancellation**: The authority decision depends on current ancestor state and the order of admission, not on whether a child has acknowledged a message. {#tbl-vol3-gateway-epoch-table}\n\nEnforcing those ancestor cancellation semantics requires an unambiguous, linearizable ordering point. Under a strict freshness policy, revocation updates and admission checks share an authoritative global order: once a revocation is committed, no subsequent admission check within that scope may evaluate against stale policy state. Because local signature verification and expiry checks establish only that a grant was valid when issued, a replicated gateway that cannot verify current ancestor state must fail closed, withholding admission from new operations.\n\nAlternatively, systems that prioritize availability over strict consistency may permit distributed gateways to evaluate requests against cached ancestor state for a declared interval. This engineering trade-off introduces a **stale-policy window**, the bounded interval in which an already-revoked grant may still be accepted by an out-of-date gateway. The system designer must explicitly bound this window to account for cache invalidation propagation, token refresh intervals, clock skew assumptions, and network partition timeouts. Revocation must also actively sever all mediation pathways, including revoking open writable file handles held by running processes. Any operations already admitted to external systems prior to revocation remain governed by transactional settlement rules.\n\n### Approval requests and operation binding {#sec-vol3-multiagent-approval-binding}\n\nDuring execution, a worker may discover that completing its objective requires an operation that exceeds its delegated authority. Rather than terminating or attempting privilege escalation, the worker submits an authorization request. An approval binds to the exact operation it authorizes, never to a model&#x27;s explanation of it (@sec-vol3-security-action-bound-approval), and the runtime recomputes that binding and rechecks its preconditions at dispatch (@sec-vol3-agent-harness-dispatch-rechecks). For a delegated publication, the bound operation names the originating task (`migration_task_01`), the content hash of the candidate artifact, the destination reference, and its base-version preconditions, so a candidate altered by a single byte after approval needs a fresh decision.\n\nDelegation changes where the request goes and what the recheck must cover. The worker cannot widen its own grant, and attenuation forbids its parent from granting more than the parent holds. The runtime therefore collects such requests in a bounded approval queue, deduplicates repeated submissions from siblings, and routes each to the task owner rather than to an intermediate parent whose own grant could not cover the operation. Any rationale a root or worker model attaches remains unverified commentary. At dispatch, the recheck extends from the operation to its lineage: current grants, every ancestor&#x27;s lineage generation, and the remaining budget must still permit the action, so an ancestor cancellation committed after approval blocks it as surely as a changed candidate does. Passing validation tests in `migration_accept_v1`, obtaining operator approval, and executing the privileged publication remain three distinct, non-fungible events.\n\n::: {#chk-17-bounding-delegation .callout-checkpoint title=&quot;Bounding work, authority, and spending after a split&quot;}\n\n- [ ] **Cancellation scope**: The supervisor cancels `migration_task_01` after the gateway admitted `worker_interface_01`&#x27;s test operation but before its reply arrives. Which statements can the runtime make truthfully now, and which must wait for further evidence?\n- [ ] **Barrier vs. quorum**: At a per-worker on-time probability of `[FanoutCompletion.admissible_str]`, why can a `[FanoutCompletion.required_str]`-of-`[FanoutCompletion.workers_str]` stopping rule rescue a read-only analysis but not the two-part migration?\n- [ ] **Hedging a mutation**: Why is a hedged copy of a mutating tool call a retry before settlement, and what must both attempts share before the runtime may issue it?\n- [ ] **Credit window**: The reviewer drains `[RateMismatch.service_rate_str]` items per second. What window keeps the modeled mean residence under one minute, and which messages must travel outside the data credits?\n- [ ] **Joint allowances and revocation**: Two siblings each receive less than the parent&#x27;s remaining allowance. What must the runtime do so they cannot jointly overdraw it, and what happens at a remote gateway that cannot confirm an ancestor&#x27;s current generation?\n\n:::\n\nDelegation controls conserve authority and spending, distinguish cancellation from completed cleanup, and separate administrative approval from verified success. These safeguards make task decomposition governable. Whether splitting a task across multiple agents is actually useful, however, remains a separate empirical question, answerable only by measurement against a strong single-agent baseline.\n\n## Benchmarking Against a Single Agent {#sec-vol3-multiagent-evaluation}\n\nWhen benchmarking the authentication migration (`migration_task_01`), granting parallel tool execution to the multi-agent supervisor while artificially restricting the single controller to serial execution measures a baseline handicap rather than the true benefit of delegation. A rigorous evaluation compares the supervisor against a single controller that already dispatches parallel searches and tests, beginning with an objective declared in advance, such as minimizing cost per accepted task subject to a deadline, or maximizing completed tasks within a fixed operating budget. The coordination tax (principle \\ref{pri-vol3-coordination-tax}) then appears in three measurements read together: accepted-task rate, elapsed wall-clock time, and cost per accepted task. While a Pareto improvement across all three dimensions is ideal, it is rarely achieved; delegating work across agents typically trades higher computational cost for lower latency. This trade-off is justified only when the engineering objective values latency reduction and the solution satisfies all quality and budget constraints.\n\n### Matched comparison conditions {#sec-vol3-multiagent-matched-conditions}\n\nComparing a multi-agent fleet against a single-turn prompt or a controller with lower tool concurrency credits delegation for resources the single agent was never allowed to use. A fair evaluation applies the matched-budget comparison established in @sec-vol3-evaluation-matched-budgets, which fixes the task distribution, criteria, and resource envelope before policies are compared. It also uses an equally capable baseline, an iterative single controller with parallel tool execution, rather than an isolated, single-turn model call. For delegation, the matched envelope must also fix starting fixtures, evidence access, and identical concurrency limits on both model calls and tool executions. Because either policy may terminate early, unconsumed budget represents an operational outcome rather than an experimental discrepancy. If a delegated design also alters underlying model selections or prompt formulations, the trial evaluates a compound bundle of variables, so isolating the architectural impact of delegation requires a controlled ablation. Finally, acceptance must be scored on the integrated end-to-end task rather than on worker subtasks, because an interface update that passes its local unit tests still fails the migration if the accompanying caller patch introduces breaking signature incompatibilities.\n\n### Outcome, latency, and cost metrics {#sec-vol3-multiagent-outcome-latency-cost}\n\nEvery attempted task must remain in the evaluation denominator; tasks that encounter timeouts, unhandled errors, or indeterminate verdicts cannot be discarded from the benchmark. When the operational contract treats late delivery as an outage, on-time acceptance must be recorded as a distinct metric alongside gross completion. End-to-end latency spans the full execution envelope, measured continuously from task arrival through task decomposition, sub-agent dispatch, intermediate reconciliation, final integration, and automated assessment. Calculating median latency solely over accepted tasks introduces severe survival bias by filtering out prolonged failures. Systems evaluation therefore tracks latency across all arrivals. A task still running at the observation cutoff is *right-censored*: its latency is known only to exceed the cutoff, so it enters the latency distribution as a lower bound rather than being dropped. This is the same refusal to discard unresolved outcomes that keeps pending and unknown verdicts in the evaluation denominator (@sec-vol3-evaluation-contract-verdicts).\n\nAlongside outcome and latency tracking, cost accounting must encompass the complete system boundary: supervisor orchestration, retrieval queries, verification suites, merge conflict reconciliation, failed exploratory attempts, canceled speculative branches, and environment cleanup. Token expenditures must distinguish input and output tokens at their respective market prices, while concurrent tool invocations must be billed by aggregate worker execution time, which frequently exceeds the wall-clock span of the critical path. The effective cost per accepted task divides this full cohort spend by the accepted count under the same matched-budget accounting, so an architecture with no accepted solutions still reports its spend and the zero count. The token inflation documented in war story \\ref{ws-vol3-multiagent-research-fanout-2025} illustrates why rigorous cost accounting is needed before committing to a multi-agent deployment.\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ TOKEN MULTIPLE VS. ACCEPTANCE: BREAK-EVEN FOR DELEGATION (LEGO)</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: notebook \\ref{nbk-17-token-multiplier-break-even}, after the war story.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal: Find the acceptance gain delegation needs to hold cost per accepted task.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show: Token ratio, the baseline acceptance above which break-even is</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│       impossible, and the cost ratio if the reported gain were acceptance.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How: C_eff = E[C_attempt]/p_acc with cost proportional to tokens.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Note: Applies ResearchReport's quoted multiples through</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│       Scenarios.ResearchSystemTokenBreakEven; the fixed price per token</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│       and gain-as-acceptance mapping are assumptions, not report claims.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import Scenarios from mlsysim.fmt import check, fmt_multiple, fmt_percent, fmt_ratio</p>\n<p>class TokenBreakEven:</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Quoted multiples; assumed fixed price per token) ────────</h1>\n<p>break_even = Scenarios.ResearchSystemTokenBreakEven multi_vs_chat = break_even.multi_agent_tokens_vs_chat single_vs_chat = break_even.agent_tokens_vs_chat eval_gain = break_even.reported_gain</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Cost ratio of C_eff = token ratio x p_single / p_multi) ─</h1>\n<p>token_ratio = break_even.token_multiple max_single_acceptance = break_even.max_single_acceptance  # p_multi &lt;= 1 caps the baseline acceptance_multiplier = break_even.acceptance_multiplier c_eff_ratio_if_gain_is_acceptance = break_even.cost_ratio_if_gain_is_acceptance</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(token_ratio &gt; 1, \"Delegation must use more tokens in this scenario\") check(0 &lt; max_single_acceptance &lt; 1, \"Break-even baseline must be a probability\") check(c_eff_ratio_if_gain_is_acceptance &gt; 1, \"The reported gain does not offset the token multiple\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>token_ratio_mult_str = fmt_multiple(token_ratio, precision=2) max_single_acceptance_str = fmt_percent(max_single_acceptance, precision=1, style=\"prose\") acceptance_multiplier_mult_str = fmt_multiple(acceptance_multiplier, precision=2) c_eff_ratio_mult_str = fmt_multiple(c_eff_ratio_if_gain_is_acceptance, precision=1) ]```</p>\n<p><strong>Problem</strong>: A delegated multi-agent architecture consumes tokens at the ratios reported for the research system: <code>[ResearchReport.multi_vs_chat_mult_str]</code> the token volume of a standard chat interaction, compared to <code>[ResearchReport.single_vs_chat_mult_str]</code> for an autonomous single agent. Assuming a constant price per token, how much must task acceptance improve for delegation to break even on cost per accepted task, and over which baseline success rates is break-even mathematically achievable?</p>\n<p><strong>Math</strong>: Let $\\DelegationTokenMultiple$ be the token multiple, delegated tokens per attempt over single-agent tokens per attempt. Following @eq-vol3-cost-per-accepted-task, with cost proportional to tokens, the delegated cost per accepted task divided by the single-agent cost per accepted task is $\\DelegationTokenMultiple\\,\\SingleAgentAcceptanceProbability/\\MultiAgentAcceptanceProbability$. With $\\DelegationTokenMultiple=$ <code>[ResearchReport.multi_vs_chat_mult_str]</code> $/$ <code>[ResearchReport.single_vs_chat_mult_str]</code> $=$ <code>[TokenBreakEven.token_ratio_mult_str]</code>, economic break-even requires that $\\MultiAgentAcceptanceProbability =$ <code>[TokenBreakEven.token_ratio_mult_str]</code> $\\cdot\\, \\SingleAgentAcceptanceProbability$. Because probability is bounded ($\\MultiAgentAcceptanceProbability \\le 1$), break-even is possible only when the single-agent baseline achieves $\\SingleAgentAcceptanceProbability \\le$ <code>[TokenBreakEven.max_single_acceptance_str]</code>. Even if the reported evaluation score gain of <code>[ResearchReport.eval_gain_str]</code> translated directly into an acceptance rate multiplier of <code>[TokenBreakEven.acceptance_multiplier_mult_str]</code>, the effective cost per accepted task would remain <code>[TokenBreakEven.c_eff_ratio_mult_str]</code> that of the single-agent baseline.</p>\n<p><strong>Systems insight</strong>: When delegation multiplies token consumption by a factor of $\\DelegationTokenMultiple$, it reduces effective cost $\\CostPerAcceptedTask$ if and only if it scales task acceptance by more than $\\DelegationTokenMultiple$. At an expansion ratio of <code>[TokenBreakEven.token_ratio_mult_str]</code>, this economic efficiency can materialize only on difficult tasks where the single agent succeeds less than <code>[TokenBreakEven.max_single_acceptance_str]</code> of the time. On workloads where a single agent already succeeds reliably, delegation cannot be justified by cost efficiency. It must be justified as a purchase of reduced wall-clock latency or broader search coverage, valued explicitly by the system's operational requirements.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h3\">Scaling models for worker count</h3>\n<p>A matched trial is expensive, so before running one a team must decide which worker counts deserve a trial at all. The fixed-work scaling model of @eq-vol3-multiagent-speedup does not replace that benchmarking; its role is to guide the search by identifying promising worker counts. Varying the worker count alters resource contention, sub-task dispatch boundaries, and inter-agent reconciliation costs simultaneously. Any empirical overhead curve therefore holds strictly within the evaluated range and serves as a hypothesis beyond it. To reason about scaling limits systematically, classical systems engineering separates everything that keeps a fleet from ideal division into work that serializes (undivided planning, and any overhead that queues at a single resource) and pairwise reconciliation. These two mechanisms require different architectural remedies.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ THE SCREEN AS A UNIVERSAL SCALABILITY LAW FIT (LEGO)\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: @eq-vol3-multiagent-usl and @eq-vol3-multiagent-usl-peak in\n# │          @sec-vol3-multiagent-evaluation.\n# │ Goal: Recast the earlier screening assumptions as USL coefficients.\n# │ Show: sigma, kappa, the continuous peak, the retrograde threshold, and\n# │       agreement between the USL and the earlier screen&#x27;s integer optimum.\n# │ How: sigma = 1 - f and kappa = per-worker overhead from DelegationScreen\n# │      (calc-vol3-delegation-screen), as Scenarios.RepairPartitionScalability;\n# │      evaluate the USL on integer counts.\n# │ Note: Reads DelegationScreen inputs only; hypothetical coefficients.\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import Scenarios\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import check, fmt_int, fmt_ratio\n\nclass UslScreen:\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Coefficients implied by the earlier screen) ─────────────\n    law = Scenarios.RepairPartitionScalability\n    sigma = law.contention\n    kappa = law.coherency\n    search_limit = law.search_limit\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (Peak, retrograde threshold, integer optimum) ─────────\n    peak = law.continuous_peak\n    retrograde = law.retrograde_threshold\n    capacity = law.capacity\n    best_workers = law.best_workers\n    first_slower = law.first_slower\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Prose claims) ─────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(best_workers == DelegationScreen.best_workers, &quot;USL optimum must match the screen&#x27;s integer optimum&quot;)\n    check(abs(capacity[best_workers] - 1 / DelegationScreen.best_time) &lt; 1e-12, &quot;USL capacity must equal the screen&#x27;s inverse time&quot;)\n    check(6.3 &lt; peak &lt; 6.4, &quot;Continuous peak drifted&quot;)\n    check(first_slower == int(retrograde) + 1, &quot;First slower count must follow the retrograde threshold&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────\n    sigma_str = fmt_ratio(sigma, precision=2)\n    kappa_str = fmt_ratio(kappa, precision=2)\n    peak_str = fmt_ratio(peak, precision=1)\n    best_workers_str = fmt_int(best_workers)\n    first_slower_str = fmt_int(first_slower)\n]```\n\n::: {#thrm-vol3-universal-scalability-law .callout-theorem title=&quot;Universal scalability law&quot;}\n\n*For a fixed amount of work divided among $\\WorkerCount$ equally capable workers,* Gunther&#x27;s **universal scalability law** (USL) [@gunther2008general] gives the relative capacity, the speedup over one worker, as @eq-vol3-multiagent-usl, with its peak at @eq-vol3-multiagent-usl-peak.\n\n$$\n\\text{Speedup}(\\WorkerCount)=\\frac{\\WorkerCount}{1+\\ContentionCoefficient(\\WorkerCount-1)+\\CoherencyCoefficient \\WorkerCount(\\WorkerCount-1)}\n$$ {#eq-vol3-multiagent-usl}\n\n$$\n\\WorkerCount^*=\\sqrt{\\frac{1-\\ContentionCoefficient}{\\CoherencyCoefficient}}\n$$ {#eq-vol3-multiagent-usl-peak}\n\n*   **Contention** ($\\ContentionCoefficient$): The share of work that serializes, including undivided planning, final assessment, and queueing at single resources such as the supervisor or the integration queue.\n*   **Coherency** ($\\CoherencyCoefficient$): The cost of pairwise reconciliation. Each of the $\\WorkerCount(\\WorkerCount-1)$ ordered worker pairs may exchange state, re-read a peer&#x27;s output, or resolve a conflicting change, the same count as the mesh round in @sec-vol3-multiagent-topologies.\n*   **Amdahl&#x27;s law** ($\\CoherencyCoefficient=0$): Capacity saturates at $1/\\ContentionCoefficient$ and never declines.\n*   **Retrograde region** ($\\WorkerCount&gt;(1-\\ContentionCoefficient)/\\CoherencyCoefficient$): A positive $\\CoherencyCoefficient$ makes added workers harmful, not merely useless. Capacity peaks at $\\WorkerCount^*$ and falls below one, slower than a single worker, past this count. With the screening notebook&#x27;s $\\ContentionCoefficient=$ `[UslScreen.sigma_str]` and $\\CoherencyCoefficient=$ `[UslScreen.kappa_str]`, every count from `[UslScreen.first_slower_str]` workers up finishes later than the serial reference.\n\n:::\n\nDividing both the numerator and denominator of @eq-vol3-multiagent-usl by $\\WorkerCount$ yields $1/[\\ContentionCoefficient+(1-\\ContentionCoefficient)/\\WorkerCount+\\CoherencyCoefficient(\\WorkerCount-1)]$. This expression matches @eq-vol3-multiagent-speedup directly when setting the parallel fraction $\\ParallelFraction=1-\\ContentionCoefficient$ and the overhead function $\\CoordinationOverhead(\\WorkerCount)=\\CoherencyCoefficient(\\WorkerCount-1)$. In elapsed-time terms, $\\ContentionCoefficient$ captures fixed serial work whose duration does not change as workers are added, whereas the screen&#x27;s linear penalty $\\WorkerOverheadSlope$ plays the role of $\\CoherencyCoefficient$ by capturing any elapsed delay that grows in proportion to $\\WorkerCount-1$. Multiplying top and bottom by $\\WorkerCount$ turns that linear elapsed-time penalty into the quadratic term $\\CoherencyCoefficient\\WorkerCount(\\WorkerCount-1)$ in the denominator of @eq-vol3-multiagent-usl. Two very different physical mechanisms can produce that same algebraic term. True pairwise state reconciliation generates $\\WorkerCount(\\WorkerCount-1)$ interactions across the fleet, and when that quadratic aggregate work is shared evenly across the $\\WorkerCount$ concurrent workers, each worker experiences an elapsed delay proportional to $\\WorkerCount-1$. However, a central supervisor that sequentially processes one extra dispatch or receipt for each additional worker also adds an elapsed delay proportional to $\\WorkerCount-1$, without any peer-to-peer communication at all. A fitted value of $\\CoherencyCoefficient$ therefore flags an elapsed overhead that scales with worker count, and engineers must still inspect traces to see whether the culprit is pairwise worker reconciliation or per-worker queuing at the coordinator. Applying the notebook&#x27;s assumed parameters, @eq-vol3-multiagent-usl-peak places the continuous theoretical optimum at `[UslScreen.peak_str]` workers, which evaluates to the discrete optimum of `[UslScreen.best_workers_str]` workers identified in the screening notebook.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↪ The stationary-root analysis that locates the peak of a fixed partition&#x27;s speedup curve is developed in @sec-vol3-appendix-math-amdahl.\n:::\n\nThe architectural remedy depends on the measured source of overhead. Supervisor queue time, dependency invalidations, and reconciliation work help distinguish serial processing from cross-worker dependency work, and a matched trial that changes one mechanism can test the diagnosis. A verified serial bottleneck may benefit from pipelining supervisory prompts, sharding shared integration queues, or asynchronous event loops. When dependency reconciliation dominates, a partition that reduces cross-agent synchronization may instead pay. Interface refactoring coupled to caller implementations provides one such dependency hazard; a research query with independent search paths provides a contrasting partition.\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ USL COEFFICIENTS BY WORKLOAD: RESEARCH VS. REPAIR (LEGO)</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: lighthouse \\ref{lhs-vol3-research-agent-fanout}.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal: Show that the workload's partition, through kappa, sets useful fan-out.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show: Research and repair optima and capacity at each optimum.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How: USL capacity evaluated on integer worker counts.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Note: Research coefficients come from Agents.Research.OrchestratorWorker</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│       through Scenarios.ResearchFanoutScalability (teaching assumptions,</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│       not measurements); repair coefficients reuse UslScreen.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import Scenarios from mlsysim.fmt import check, fmt_int, fmt_multiple, fmt_ratio</p>\n<p>class ResearchLighthouse:</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Scenario coefficients) ──────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>research_law = Scenarios.ResearchFanoutScalability repair_law = UslScreen.law research_sigma = research_law.contention  # lead agent's planning and synthesis, assumed research_kappa = research_law.coherency  # rare cross-subagent reconciliation, assumed repair_sigma = UslScreen.sigma repair_kappa = UslScreen.kappa search_limit = research_law.search_limit</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Integer optima and capacities) ───────────────────────</h1>\n<p>research_best = research_law.best_workers repair_best = repair_law.best_workers research_capacity = research_law.peak_capacity repair_capacity = repair_law.peak_capacity fanout_ratio = research_best / repair_best</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(repair_best == UslScreen.best_workers, \"Repair optimum must match the screen\") check(research_best &gt; 3 * repair_best, \"Research partition should tolerate a much wider fan-out\") check(research_capacity &gt; repair_capacity, \"Lower coherency should raise peak capacity\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>research_sigma_str = fmt_ratio(research_sigma, precision=2) research_kappa_str = fmt_ratio(research_kappa, precision=3) research_best_str = fmt_int(research_best) research_capacity_mult_str = fmt_multiple(research_capacity, precision=1) repair_best_str = fmt_int(repair_best) repair_capacity_mult_str = fmt_multiple(repair_capacity, precision=1) fanout_ratio_mult_str = fmt_multiple(fanout_ratio, precision=1) ]```</p>\n<p><strong>Workload</strong>: The research agent addresses open-ended, breadth-first queries. A lead orchestrator decomposes the inquiry into independent search paths, dispatches read-only subagents to retrieve and analyze external sources, and synthesizes their condensed findings. The builders of one production research system report that this breadth-first regime is where delegation helps most, in contrast to coding tasks whose tight interdependencies leave less work to run in parallel [@anthropic2025multiagent].</p>\n<p><strong>Scenario coefficients</strong>: Suppose lead-agent planning and final synthesis serialize $\\ContentionCoefficient=$ <code>[ResearchLighthouse.research_sigma_str]</code> of total execution, while subagents operate almost entirely without cross-communication ($\\CoherencyCoefficient=$ <code>[ResearchLighthouse.research_kappa_str]</code>). Evaluating the scalability model yields an optimal fan-out of <code>[ResearchLighthouse.research_best_str]</code> subagents achieving <code>[ResearchLighthouse.research_capacity_mult_str]</code> relative capacity. In contrast, the code repair agent's high coherency limits its configuration to <code>[ResearchLighthouse.repair_best_str]</code> workers at <code>[ResearchLighthouse.repair_capacity_mult_str]</code> capacity. Under these assumptions, the research partition permits a <code>[ResearchLighthouse.fanout_ratio_mult_str]</code> wider effective worker fan-out. (These stylized coefficients illustrate the scaling regime rather than measured telemetry.)</p>\n<p><strong>Local constraint</strong>: Low coherency overhead permits wider fan-out under the supplied model, but token expenditure and context trust can still constrain deployment. At fixed per-worker work, token consumption scales linearly with fan-out, so the economic break-even threshold analyzed in notebook \\ref{nbk-17-token-multiplier-break-even} may cap deployment before the modeled capacity peak $\\WorkerCount^*$. Because worker receipts also incorporate uncurated text from external web APIs, the bounded receipt protocol of @sec-vol3-multiagent-contracts limits returned context and keeps worker claims separate from runtime evidence. Source-derived fields and summaries remain untrusted inputs whose proposed uses require the security checks established for the tool boundary.</p>\n<p><strong>Systems insight</strong>: Under the supplied coefficients, the research partition supports wider fan-out than the repair partition. Dependency traces can establish whether interface isolation addresses the repair workload's overhead; matched outcome and cost measurements determine whether wider research fan-out earns its additional spend. Admission controls bound that spend independently of the modeled capacity gain.</p>\n<p>In production systems, empirical engineering inverts this analytical workflow. Rather than postulating coefficients, engineers conduct targeted benchmarking trials across a small set of practical worker counts (for example, $\\WorkerCount \\in \\{1, 2, 4\\}$) to estimate $\\ContentionCoefficient$ and $\\CoherencyCoefficient$. The resulting model fit projects the empirical capacity peak $\\WorkerCount^*$, identifying the next candidate configuration to benchmark. A large fitted coherency parameter $\\CoherencyCoefficient$ warrants inspecting traces and testing targeted interventions. Redesigning the task decomposition is justified when that evidence confirms that dependency reconciliation dominates; the coefficient alone does not establish the remedy.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h3\">A complete delegation decision</h3>\n<p>Once the scalability fit has nominated a configuration, the matched trial decides whether it earns its tax. Consider the evaluation results for a representative cohort of software migration tasks patterned after <code>migration_task_01</code>, evaluated under identical automated test suites (<code>migration_accept_v1</code>). Both configurations operate under identical constraints: matching tool and model concurrency limits, identical sandbox permissions, and equivalent token and financial ceilings. The experiment isolates the delegation architecture itself (how work is decomposed, dispatched, and integrated) rather than what the runtime allows.</p>", "quizzes": []};
